/* ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   English translations of the passages principle-search.html finds: every
   passage in which a form in principles-lexicon.js occurs, as of 23 October 2026.

   These are MACHINE TRANSLATIONS by Claude (Anthropic), made once, passage by
   passage, and NOT REVIEWED by a scholar. Check the original before quoting.
   The page labels them so wherever it shows them.

   How they were made:
     - German and Latin alike are translated into English. The first time a
       principle is named in a passage, its original name follows in parentheses:
       "the principle of contradiction (principium contradictionis)".
     - The Academy Edition's apparatus is carried through as printed: square
       brackets, Kant's additions (g …) and (s …), < bricht ab >, and Baumgarten's
       cross-references (§. 14). Words Kant struck out, in square brackets, are
       sometimes left in his German or Latin.
     - Baumgarten's German glosses stand as [German gloss: … — English].
     - ⟨ ⟩ marks something the translation adds that is not in the text: a
       word supplied to complete a sentence, or a note on a suspected misprint.
     - Fragments are translated as fragments; nothing missing is invented.

   PRINCIPLE_TRANSLATIONS.en is keyed by the page's passage keys:
     met:<§>            Baumgarten, Metaphysica (data/metaphysica-17.js)
     meier:<§>          Meier, Auszug (meier-reader/meier-reading-guide.html)
     refl:<r>, reflm:<r>  a Reflexion, by Adickes' number
     blomberg:<block id>, wiener:<block id>, busolt:<block id>
                        a block of data/logik-*-24.js

   A passage the lexicon comes to find later has no entry here; the page then says
   so and offers a link to an outside translator instead. To add or correct one,
   edit its entry directly.
   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */

const PRINCIPLE_TRANSLATIONS = {
  meta: {
    label: 'machine translation by Claude, not reviewed by a scholar',
    made: '2026-10-23',
    passages: 169,
  },
  en: {
    "met:7": "The negative nothing (cf. §. 54), the unrepresentable, the impossible, the repugnant, (the absurd, cf. §. 13,) that which involves a contradiction, the implicating, the contradictory, is A and non-A; or, there is no subject of contradictory predicates; or, nothing both is and is not. 0 = A + non-A. This proposition is called the principle of contradiction (principium contradictionis), and is absolutely first.",
    "met:10": "Every possible is either A, or non-A, or neither, §. 8; but neither is nothing, because it would be both, §. 9. Therefore every possible either is A or is non-A; or, of all contradictory predicates, one or the other belongs to every subject. This proposition ⟨the volume prints praepositio, probably a misprint for propositio⟩ is called the principle of the excluded third (principium exclusi tertii), or of the excluded middle (medii), between two contradictories.",
    "met:11": "Every possible A is A; or, whatever is, is that; or, every subject is a predicate of itself. If you deny this: some possible A is non-A, §. 10, hence A and non-A, or nothing, §. 7, which is impossible, §. 9. This proposition is called the principle of position (principium positionis), or of identity (identitatis).",
    "met:20": "Every possible either has a ground or does not, §. 10. If it has a ground, something is its ground, §. 8. If it does not, nothing is its ground, §. 7. Therefore the ground of every possible is either nothing or something, §. 10. If nothing were the ground of some possible, it would be cognizable from nothing why that possible is, §. 14; hence nothing itself would be representable and something, §. 8, nothing would be something, §. 14, 8. Hence some possible would be impossible, §. 7, 8, which is absurd (q.a.), §. 9. Therefore something is the ground of every possible; or, every possible is a consequence (rationatum); or, nothing is without a ground; or, when something is posited, something is posited as its ground. This proposition is called the principle of ground (principium rationis), which you may also gather from §. 265, 279, partly by abstracting, partly by avoiding a circle.",
    "met:22": "Nothing is without a sufficient ground; or, when something is posited, something is posited as its sufficient ground. Each of the things in every possible has a ground, §. 20; hence every possible has a sufficient ground, §. 21. This proposition is called the principle of sufficient ground (principium rationis sufficientis) (of fittingness [convenientiae]).",
    "met:23": "Every possible is a ground; or, nothing is without a consequence, nothing without a corollary and an outcome, nothing at all sterile, idle and unfruitful; or, when something is posited, something is posited as its consequence. For every possible either has a consequence or does not, §. 10. If it has one, something is its consequence, §. 8; if it does not, nothing is its consequence, §. 7. Therefore the consequence of every possible is either nothing or something, §. 10. If nothing were the consequence of some possible, it could be cognized from this, §. 14; hence it would be something, §. 8, and so some possible would be impossible, §. 7, 8, which is absurd (q.a.), §. 9. Let this proposition be called the principle of the consequence (principium rationati).",
    "met:24": "Every possible is a ground and a consequence, §. 20, 23; hence it is connected by a twofold nexus, §. 14, and rational, §. 19, cognizable both a priori and a posteriori. Let this proposition be called the principle of things connected on both sides (principium utrimque connexorum) (on the side of what precedes, and on the side of what follows).",
    "met:30": "When the ground is posited, and hence also the sufficient ground, §. 21, the consequence is posited, §. 23; or, from the ground, and the sufficient ground, to the consequence the inference is valid (V. C.).",
    "met:31": "When the ground, and the sufficient ground, is removed, some consequence is removed, because if this were posited, that would also be posited, §. 29.",
    "met:57": "Everything actual is internally possible, §. 54; or, when existence is posited, internal possibility is posited, §. 55, 40; from being to being possible the inference is valid (ab esse ad posse V. C.).",
    "met:90": "Since the determinations of every being are conjoined—the essential ones according to the principle of contradiction (principium contradictionis), §. 40, 7, and the accidental ones: the attributes according to the principle of contradiction, §. 64, 7, and of ground (rationis), §. 20, and of sufficient ground (sufficientis), §. 22, 50, the modes according to the principle of contradiction, §. 65, 7, and of ground, §. 42, 20, the essentials and affections according to the principle of the consequence (principium rationati), §. 23, 41—hence according to common rules, §. 83, 86, every being is transcendentally true, § 89.",
    "met:114": "Necessary things are determinable in only one way and manner. For they are either A or non-A. A third way, a third manner of determinability, is impossible, §. 10. Now suppose that a necessary thing is determinable through A; non-A is the opposite of this determination, §. 81, hence impossible, §. 101. There are therefore not several ways and manners of determinability besides the single A, §. 77. Let things determinable in only one way and manner be A: their opposite would be non-A, §. 81, but this is impossible, §. 77. Let them be non-A: their opposite would be A, §. 81, but this is impossible, §. 77. There is no third (tertium non datur), §. 10. Therefore the opposite of things determinable in only one way and manner is impossible, and so they themselves are necessary, §. 102. Contingent things are not necessary, hence they are neither determinable in only one way and manner, nor in more than two, §. 10. They are therefore determinable in a twofold way. Things determinable in a twofold way and manner are not necessary, hence contingent, §. 102. Thus there can be defined 1) the necessary, through determinable in only one way, 2) the contingent, through determinable in a twofold way, 3) necessity, through the singleness, 4) contingency, through the twofoldness of determinability.",
    "met:151": "The determinations of a lower being which are undetermined in its higher being are its difference [German gloss: die genauere Bestimmung — the more precise determination]. Hence the generic difference [German gloss: eines Geschlechtes — of a genus] is the complex of the determinations determined in the genus, undetermined in its higher one. The specific difference [German gloss: einer Art — of a species] is the complex of the determinations of the species undetermined in its lowest genus. The numerical difference [German gloss: eines jeden einzeln — of each one singly] (haecceity, the principle of individuation [principium individuationis]) is the complex of the determinations of the individual undetermined in the species, §. 148.",
    "met:268": "Since there is a partial similarity of all beings, §. 265, beings are not totally diverse, §. 267. Let us call this proposition the principle of denied total dissimilarity and diversity (principium negatae totalis dissimilitudinis et diversitatis).",
    "met:269": "The total identity of singulars is numerical. It is impossible that there be two singulars outside one another that are entirely or totally the same. For when two are posited, many are posited, hence things partly the same, partly diverse, §. 74. Therefore they are not totally the same, §. 267. Singulars that are totally the same are the same in number, and not partly the same, partly diverse, §. 267. Hence they are not many, nor two, §. 74. This proposition is called the principle of (the identity of) indiscernibles taken broadly (principium (identitatis) indiscernibilium late sumptum), or of denied total identity.",
    "met:270": "It is impossible that there be several actual things outside one another that are totally congruent. For while they existed outside one another, its own proper existence would belong to each, §. 86, diverse from the existence of the other, §. 38; hence either an attribute or a mode, at least a single one, §. 77, would be in the one that was not in the other, §. 38, 55, and so either a quality or a quantity, §. 69, and hence the two would not be totally congruent, §. 70, 267. Totally congruent things, as regards all internal differences, are the same, §. 267, 70; hence the existence of the one is not diverse from the existence of the other, §. 70; therefore they do not exist outside one another. Let this proposition be the principle of denied total congruence (principium negatae totalis congruentiae).",
    "met:271": "It is impossible that there be several actual things outside one another that are totally similar. For either they would also be totally equal, or not. If the former, they would be totally congruent, §. 70, 267, which is absurd (q.a.), §. 270. If they were not totally equal, there would be a quantity in the one that was not in the other, §. 70, 38. This would have a sufficient ground, §. 22. Hence there would be in one of the totally similar things a quality that was not in the other, §. 69, 14, which is absurd (q.a.), §. 70, 267. This proposition is the principle of (the identity of) indiscernibles taken strictly (principium (identitatis) indiscernibilium stricte sumptum), or of denied total similarity.",
    "met:272": "It is impossible that there be several actual things outside one another that are totally equal. For either they will at the same time be totally similar, or only partially, §. 265. If the former, they would be totally congruent, §. 70, 267, which is absurd (q.a.), §. 270. If they were only partially similar, there would be a quality in the one that was not in the other, §. 267, 70; hence there would not be in both totally the same degree of reality, §. 248, and so some quantity of the one would not be a quantity of the other, §. 246; therefore they would not be totally equal either, §. 267, 70. Let this proposition be the principle of denied total equality (principium negatae totalis aequalitatis).",
    "met:824": "If God were not actual, the principle of contradiction (principium contradictionis) would be false, §. 823, 7, the first principle both of the form and of the matter in all our demonstrations. Therefore, although many sciences can be demonstrated completely without any theological premise, §. 1-800, nevertheless neither they themselves nor their objects would be, e.g. §. 61, 354, 504; indeed they would not even be possible, §. 8, unless God were actual.",
    "meier:96": "The second internal mark of the truth of a cognition consists in its being possible in a connection (possibilitas cognitionis hypothetica). Consequently 1) if it is a consequence of correct grounds, and 2) a ground of correct consequences, §.94. 93. Accordingly a cognition is true if it is not impossible and is in accordance with the principle of sufficient ground (Satz des zureichenden Grundes) §.16.",
    "meier:341": "Equipollent judgments (iudicia aequipollentia) are different judgments in which the logical relation of one and the very same subject and predicate is the same. E.g. not all human beings are virtuous, some human beings are not virtuous; not no human being is learned, some human beings are learned; everything has a ground, nothing is without a ground. Now since the truth and incorrectness of a judgment are found in the logical relation §.295. 296, 1) all the other equipollent judgments are true if one is true; and 2) all the others false if one is false. The equipollence of judgments depends on such alterations of the judgment as do not change its truth or incorrectness; otherwise they are not equipollent judgments.",
    "meier:362": "It is impossible that something should at the same time be and not be. Or, if one and the very same thing is at the same time affirmed or denied of a thing, then it is nothing. This proposition is called the principle of contradiction (Satz des Widerspruchs; principium contradictionis), and it is the first internal mark of truth §.95, on which all conclusions of syllogisms (Vernunftschlüsse) must rest if they are to be true §.361.",
    "meier:363": "Whatever can with truth be universally affirmed or denied of a concept can also with truth be affirmed or denied of every other concept that falls under it §.342. Otherwise two contradictory judgments would have to be true at once §.343, and that is contrary to the principle of contradiction (Satz des Widerspruchs) §.362. This proposition is called the inference from the universal to the particular (dictum de omni et nullo — what is said of all and of none).",
    "reflm:3709": "Prolegomena of the metaphysicians (Prolegomena Metaphysicorum). In our cognition as a whole, some cognitions must lie at the ground of others. Other concepts lie at the ground of many concepts, and other judgments at the ground of many judgments. The concept of time, of a motion, and of measurement lies at the ground of the commonest concept of a clock. Whoever names the word friendship relies on the concepts of love, of honesty, etc. It is just the same with judgments. Whoever says that envy is a vice grounds himself secretly on many hidden judgments: that love of one's neighbour is a duty, that whatever is contrary to a duty is a vice, that envy is ill-will and contrary to love, etc. One can call the fundamental concepts notiones fundamentales, the fundamental judgments judicia fundamentalia. Those fundamental concepts that do not in turn presuppose others are called notiones primitivae (first fundamental concepts), and judgments of that kind judicia primitiva (first fundamental judgments). But something can be regarded as a first either absolutely or relatively to something else. Something is a cognitio absolute primitiva (absolutely primitive cognition) insofar as no other at all lies at its ground; but it is respective primitiva (relatively primitive) insofar as [not in accordance with a certain rational purpose] it either is not within the powers of a subject to cognize its ground, or it is not in accordance with a certain rational purpose. Much lies at the ground of the concept of good and evil, which the philosophers also clearly cognize, but the common man surely cannot penetrate so far into his concepts, and for him they are notiones primitivae. It is just the same in common cognition with the concepts of space and time. Some of these fundamental concepts can be notiones primitivae with respect to the whole of human understanding, although in sensu absoluto (in the absolute sense) they may be only derivativae (derivative), e.g. being simultaneous, being one after another, etc. These one can call notiones [absolute] primitivas in sensu subjectivo (primitive concepts in the subjective sense). Whether they are so also in sensu objectivo (in the objective sense), the human being cannot determine. In the same way there are judicia in sensu subjectivo prima (judgments first in the subjective sense) with respect to some human beings, e.g. proverbs; or with respect to all, e.g. the principle of contradiction (principium contradictionis). Now if we call all cognitions that lie at the ground of others principia (principles), then there are respectus (respects) with regard to bricht ab.",
    "reflm:3710": "All (g first) principles are either formal or material. The first contain the ground of how the concepts in the judgment are to be considered in relation. The second contain the medium terminum (middle term) by means of which they are to be considered with one another in this relation. 1. The way in which the judgments are to be compared is either through affirmation or negation. An affirmative judgment represents that a predicate is identical with the subject (is its mark). A negative one, that the predicate contradicts the subject (is an opposite of its mark). The universal formal principle of the former can thus only be this one, which indicates the essence of affirmation: cuilibet subjecto competit praedicatum ipsi identicum (to every subject belongs the predicate identical with it), and of the second the one that contains the essence of a negation: [cuilibet subjecto] nulli subjecto competit praedicatum ipsi oppositum ([to every subject] to no subject belongs the predicate opposed to it). The first is the principle of identity (Satz der identitaet), the second that of contradiction (des Wiederspruchs). From these principles alone nothing at all can be proved. For to prove means to judge mediately, or to infer. But every syllogism (Vernunftschlus) is a judgment by means of an intermediate mark (nota intermedia, medius terminus,). But the said propositions contain no medium terminum to others. All propositions that stand immediately under these two are indemonstrable; all that stand mediately under them are demonstrable. 2. Universal judgments that contain the medium terminum to others are their material grounds; and, if they stand immediately under the two highest principiis formalibus (formal principles), they are material first principles. E.g. A body is composite stands immediately under the principle of identity and contains the medius terminus to the proposition: a body is alterable; e.g. Everything composite is alterable, a body is composite, therefore it is etc. By contrast, the proposition: no space is without places, stands immediately under the principle of contradiction etc:",
    "reflm:3716": "29. S. I: A cognition of reason that has no other principia than empirical concepts can only be a critique; one can understand the universal (e.g. the moving) only from the particular, and the universal rules can only be abstracted from the particular ones of practice. So it is also with the doctrine of the probable. Truth has objective marks; but the certainty that in each case these objective ones are to be found can have only subjective ones, i.e. the agreement of cognition with itself. A universal concept is empirical if it cannot be understood other[wise than] than by cognizing its concretum. E.g. One cannot obtain a concept of water through all definitions, [nor] than by having seen it. Above all, all concepts of beauty, of simplicity, of the ridiculous are comprehensible only in their concreto. Hence here too the rules cannot give rise to practice; rather, practice makes the rules. –––––––––– In regard to the judging of the moral the question is not whether it is agreeable, but whether it is good. We do indeed cognize in it a connection, as regards possibility, with happiness, but it does not rest on that. The principles of moral judging are not to be grounds of the inclination to the good, [i.e.] for these belong to feeling. The most vicious human being, however small the moral inclination to the good may be proportionally, nevertheless holds it to be the best. The principien of this judgment are to be established. –––––––––– All sciences and arts relate either to the culture of sound reason or not. In the latter case there also prevails a complete lack of taste. The age of sound reason and of the arts and sciences is only the age of honour; this is found only in republics and monarchies, thus not in the feudal constitution, where far too great an inequality prevails. (g The principium of identity and of contradiction are for reason what space and time are for the senses. In general: the principium of agreement (whether of sameness or of difference). The principium of the highest reality is parallel to the sum total of all sensations.) Perfection is: what constitutes the worth of the person; happiness: what constitutes the worth of the condition. (The principle of sufficient ground (Satz des zureichenden Grundes) is grounded on this: that one can cancel everything in and for itself, so that, in order to posit something, something else must be posited with which this cancelling conflicts. But this connection is either according to laws of reason or of the senses.) But this proposition means only that nothing can be cognized according to reason without a ground. –––––––––– (g If we want to posit something through reason, we need a ground. I.e. positing a priori is through a ground, unless it were intuition.) In the proposition: everything that is has a ground, there lie the two: 1. something else, 2. through which the consequence is determined; the former dependens, the latter contingens. S. II: All ideas of metaphysics are analytic except those of space, time and force. Analysis rests on the principles of identity and of contradiction. But all these and other principles are grounded on this one: that the understanding posits nothing absolute, but only insofar as it is necessitated to do so by a condition, either analytically or synthetically. The idea of the possible. The idea of all or of a whole. Metaphysics is not a philosophy about objects, for these can only be given through the senses, but about the subject, namely its laws of reason. We have laws for the use of our reason a posteriori; these cannot be applied to concepts, but only to objects of experience. Metaphysics therefore treats only the subject dogmatically, but the object problematically with regard to synthetic judgments. All judgments are either logical, which enunciate respective being, or real, which enunciate absolute being. All being in itself [itself] (position) is an existence either respective (g hypothetice) or categorice. Universality is understood either synthetically, and then holds only of the cases of experience of the synthesis, or analytically, and then holds according to reason of concepts. To determine the bounds of reason requires something positive: first, to show the extent of the cognitions of reason; and something negative: namely the limits; finally also the quality of the bounds, as it were the figure. Cognitions are of a twofold kind: either those that concern objects that are given, or those that concern the concepts of the form in which reason considers every object. The latter are merely subjective and also alone can have a universality of reason. In synthetic judgments I represent to myself an identity for the sake of the predicate, but not the predicate for the sake of the identity. The fundamental concepts of analysis are: possibility, impossibility, necessity, contingency, unity etc.; of synthesis: space, time and force. Not all conceptus superiores (higher concepts) are abstracti (abstracted), but only those that arise a posteriori analytically; those a priori are not abstracti. These abstracti are empirical or rational.",
    "reflm:3717": "27: S. I: The principium of the form of all experiences is space and time. The principium of the form of all judgments of pure reason: identity and contradiction. The principium of the form of all judgments of reason a posteriori: ground and force. Comparison, derivation, taking together. Possible is contradistinguished: 1. from the [conflict], whose concept contains a conflict. 2. From the determined or true. 3. From the actual. In all empirical judgments the basic formula is: [everything] In each thing, that which contains the ground of one sensation is also the same subject that contains the ground of the other. Synthesis is one either of taking together or of derivation. All necessity and contingency that we can represent to ourselves is conditioned. The unconditioned is thought problematically. Absolutely contingent (e.g. free action) and absolutely necessary can neither of them be thought. Every relation is either of connection or of comparison or of taking together. All connection (in the real sense): space, time and force. All definitions are grounded on the concept of essence; hence we can define nothing but pure concepts of reason and arbitrary concepts. All abstract cognitions have first grounds a posteriori. The universal judgment that has not arisen through abstraction a posteriori, and thus empirically, is not abstract but a pure judgment of reason. In metaphysics the grounds of cognition must be taken a priori, although the objects a posteriori. If the grounds [from that] concerning what must be are taken from what happens, then they are a posteriori. Synthesis is twofold: of the coordinate and of the subordinate. The synthesis of reason or of experience. S. II: Besides the principle of sufficient ground (Satz des zureichenden Grundes) this also holds: all analysis [is presupposes a synt] conversely includes the possibility of a synthesis. Accordingly in every series of subordinated things there must be a first, because otherwise no complete synthesis and hence also no analysis would take place. The synthetic principle of the understanding is bricht ab. Mathematical analysis is always a philosophical synthesis, only that I think the whole before the parts; but if I think the parts before the whole, then it is a mathematical synthesis[in the]. All synthesis rests on coordination and happens through the understanding. Philosophical analysis (analysis philosophica), however, rests on subordination and happens through reason. The ground is not a part of the consequence, nor conversely. In all images of things I coordinate nothing but immediate marks and partial representations. ab represents [a co] the action of coordination, ac however that of subordination. The soul has three dimensions: sense and its clarity; the understanding and its form as well as matter (i.e. extensiveness) of coordination; and reason, that is, height of subordination. Might it not be the same with the relations to feeling? Because all negations serve only to prevent errors and to demonstrate not-knowing, metaphysics is a very useful science, not insofar as it extends knowledge, but insofar as it prevents errors. One learns what Socrates knew. Taste concerns appearances and not sensation, consequently the constitution of the object insofar as it is an object of the senses, and is thus, as a cognition, subject to universally valid rules. The sensible is a perfection, and confusion is not essential to it; rather, distinctness of coordination can take place. ––––––––––– Mathematics shows the greatest dignity of human reason, metaphysics however its limits and its proper vocation. It belongs absolutely to everything beautiful that I need for it only understanding and as little reason as possible; for it is to please in appearance. Hence coordination. ––––––––––– Remark: that the rules of the beautiful are given through experience, because one cannot think any beautiful shape of a rational being without a human figure.",
    "reflm:3719": "ε2? ι2? κ3? (µ? ρ2? ψ?) Beside »ebenso nothwendig — doch ich muß« (lines 15-18): If the real consequence were contained in the real ground and thereby posited according to the rule of identity (Regel der Identität), it would always be simultaneous with it. All alterations are therefore possible only through the real relation of grounds to their consequences, and logical grounds are therefore distinguished from the real ground not through the limits of my cognition but in themselves.",
    "reflm:3724": "Zuund Possibility is respective position in conformity with the principle of contradiction (principium contradictionis). Truth (affirmative) is respective position according to the principle of identity (principium identitatis). Existence is absolute position. From respective position to absolute the inference is not valid. But from respective position taken generally to absolute the inference is valid, since, unless something were absolutely posited, no respect at all would be possible.",
    "reflm:3725": "Absolute necessity is either logical: on account of the principle of contradiction (principium contradictionis), or real: not on account of the principle of contradiction. The former is the necessity of judgments, or the necessity of the relation of predicate and subject. The latter is the necessity of beings. 1. God is omnipotent. 2. God is. The latter cannot be cognized through the contradiction of the opposite (g in itself). The opposite of existing is not-being. But not-being alone does not contradict itself. Existence is not a predicate; hence its opposite is not a predicate opposed to anything.",
    "reflm:3733": "(g The thing) That which contains the ground of the inner possibility of things is absolutely necessary. Conversely, (g the thing) which is absolutely necessary contains the ground of the (g inner) possibility of things; (g for) if things were internally necessary even without it, then there would be no impossibility at all in its not being. (g Or because [ein Ding] the opposite of a thing cannot contradict itself, it must conflict with some other thing.) That which contains the ground of one inner possibility contains the ground of all. For let us suppose that something possible still remained over; then this would not agree with some possible thing, that is: it would contradict something possible; but whatever contradicts something possible is impossible. (g The necessary being therefore contains the ground of all possibility.) In all possibility the formal and the material are distinguished. The ground of the former is the principle of contradiction (principium contradictionis); of the latter, some existence that contains the ground of reality. Accordingly the most real being contains the ground of all possibility (s not a particular ground of the inner possibility of things, and another not) and is absolutely necessary. The necessary being is all-sufficient (s the most perfect transcendentally), i.e. the most real as a ground. The necessary being is a single one. For if we posit several, then each of them could be cancelled in such a way that all the others remain; but since, when something necessary is cancelled, the impossible arises, it would be possible that something possible were impossible. Moreover, each of them would be a consequence of the others: regressus in infinitum curvilineus (a curvilinear regress to infinity). The necessary being is a simple one. For if a whole is necessary, then its parts must be so too; but then there would be many necessary beings. The world is therefore not a necessary being. The necessary being is the highest good, for among all possible things goodness is a reality without which all existence is, in respect of worth, equal to nothing. Therefore it contains the ground of all good. The good, however, consists in the relation of things (g to beings) that have cognitions and feeling, [den] and a being that on that account contains the ground of things. The necessary being has the most perfect understanding and will. The necessary being is therefore a person, which contains the ground of all existence through understanding and will. I.e. it is a God.",
    "reflm:3739": "When I deny something of a subject, this happens in accordance with the principle of contradiction (Satz des Widerspruchs). This negation together with the affirmation is impossible.",
    "reflm:3741": "The material principles of judgments are notions; the formal, the principle of identity (principium identitatis) or of contradiction (contradictionis); the material principles of syllogisms (ratiocinia) are notions, the formal, the dictum de omni et nullo (the rule of all and none) (s The logical principle of judgments. The real principle of concepts.)",
    "reflm:3745": "From the principle of identity (Satz der Identität) and of contradiction there follow not merely propositions, but necessary ones.",
    "reflm:3751": "808. Not positing and cancelling are different. To posit both of two contradictorily opposed predicates at once is impossible by the principle of contradiction (principium contradictionis). To cancel both of two contradictorily opposed predicates is impossible by the principle of excluded middle (principium exclusi medii). To think neither of two contradictorily opposed predicates of something is possible: the rule of abstract notions, which are indeterminate; that the latter is possible is evident from this, that, if they are not material, there is no contradiction.",
    "reflm:3752": "[respectus] Whoever thinks a ground also thinks the consequence, and conversely: it is a concept of a relation (respectus). Since no simpler relation can be thought, the concept of it is also inexplicable. One can nevertheless prevent, by means of a judgment, ground and consequences from being regarded as entirely one and the same: so that it is said: posita ratione ponitur aliud (when the ground is posited, something else is posited); or: ratio et rationatum sunt diversa (ground and consequence are different).",
    "reflm:3762": "The respective positing of the predicates of a being in relation to one another, in conformity with the principle of contradiction (principium contradictionis), contains less in itself than the absolute positing of the same predicates together; therefore the inference from the former to the latter is not valid.",
    "reflm:3813": "It is to be proved 1. that an absolutely necessary being is possible. 2. That it is the most real being (realissimum). The absolute necessity of the thing must be derived from concepts [und nicht aus] and not from its relation to other existing things. It does not consist in the opposite's contradicting itself. No opposite of existence contradicts itself; only that of propositions. It must therefore be necessary in connection with the possible. Not as a consequence, but as a ground. Possibility is a respective positing. Much can indeed be posited as regards possibility even though it is not posited absolutely; but where nothing at all exists, there is no possibility. (g All simple possibilities contain the material ground of possibility; their possibility does not rest on the principle of contradiction (Satz des Widerspruchs).)",
    "reflm:3814": "Opposite the number of M § 815: All judgments are logical or real. The latter concern existence and, when they concern absolute necessity, cannot be cognized through the principle of contradiction (Satz des Widerspruchs).",
    "reflm:3815": "God contains the material ground of all possibility, and herein lies at the same time, according to the principle of contradiction (principium contradictionis), its formal ground. That God contains the ground of the essence of things is not an operative perfection (perfectio operativa), but a quiescent one (qviescens). Divine all-sufficiency is either internal or external all-sufficiency (omnisufficientia interna vel externa).",
    "reflm:3885": "From being to being possible the inference is valid, and it is empirical. Being possible concluded [posse] through the principle of contradiction (principium contradictionis) is cognized rationally. The possibility of a real connection we cognize empirically. From not being to not being possible the inference is not valid, but it is nevertheless an imperfect empirical conclusion.",
    "reflm:3898": "In complex concepts the inference from being possible to being is not valid, but in simple ones it is. From the form of possibility one cannot infer actuality, but one can from the matter.",
    "reflm:3922": "1145. 730. M III: Material principles seem to be: whatever [entsteht] happens must have a ground. Every successive series has a beginning*. Such propositions implicate themselves; for since the beginning is an arising [ist] or happening, there would in turn, to that extent, have to be a ground of it. The idea of freedom indicates an arising without a preceding determining ground. The nature of our understanding entails that, according to its rule, nothing (g contingent) is thinkable without connection with grounds, and that a succession (in time) without a ground, and a happening of something without its being in connection with its ground, cannot be thought, because then the understanding would be wholly unusable. M IV: *(g and every series of subordinated things has a first. †) †(s It is hard to think in general, in the series of all things, a beginning which everything else succeeds, as creation. Likewise it is impossible to think an elapsed infinite series. The idea of using \"all\" of an infinite series seems to be false, and yet the necessity of the first cause (causa prima) is grounded on it, because otherwise everything would be caused (causatum).) M III: Another synthetic principle is: that which thinks is only a simple subject. Every thing must (not absolutely, but relatively to another) be at some time, either simultaneously or successively. It does not follow that what must be judged according to the laws of our understanding is true, when it concerns such things as our understanding, by the constitution of its use, is not destined to judge. [Es wäre keine Philosophie] We borrow the law of sufficient reason (Gesetz des zureichenden Grundes) from bodily appearances; but if we want to make it universal [so] and apply it to things that are elevated above the idea of our understanding, then we confuse the idea of what is for us absolute incomprehensibility with inner impossibility.",
    "reflm:3928": "The principles of the form of all analytic judgments (g of the existential judgments of things absolutely, or of predicates relatively) are the principle of identity (Satz der Identität) and of contradiction (des Widerspruchs), and [der subiective] the proofs are conducted by showing, through the analysis of the given concept, the identity or opposition with the predicate. The principles of the form of synthetic judgments are: whatever is always connected with [dem] a known part of the possible concept of a thing belongs as a part to this concept, etc. The proposition: every body is impenetrable, is analytic, because [sich der K] not only can the body not be thought without impenetrability, but because it is thought solely through impenetrability; this mark belongs as a part (pars) to the notion of body. But: every body is inert, is a synthetic proposition; for inertia is a co-part (compars) with the concept [des K] of that which is thought under the expression \"body\", forming a whole concept which is necessarily connected with those partial concepts that belong to the notion of body. If one had the whole concept of which the notions of the subject and the predicate are co-parts (compartes), then the synthetic judgments would turn into analytic ones. The question is how far there is something arbitrary here. So much for logical connection (nexus logicus). As regards real connection (nexus realis), its material principles are experiences; the formal principles are: everything that happens has a determining ground; and secondly: everything has a first ground. These principles are both synthetic, the former for the use of our reason, the latter for the terminus of this use. For according to the former we always look, in the series of mutually determining causes, to the higher grounds, and according to the latter we admit that this series is bounded. But it is just as impossible to represent a series of subordinated grounds that has no beginning as to conceive how it begins. Nevertheless the proposition: everything that happens has a determining ground — this proposition, which makes an infinite series necessary — is the principle of the form of all our judgments of reason about real connection. But the proposition: every series of subordinated things and every successive series has a first, is a synthetic proposition which is abstracted more from the limits of our understanding than from the object of cognition. The first in the series of causes is always free choice. That this has no determining ground is an empirical proposition, but to that extent uncertain; its certainty depends rather on the fact that otherwise there could be no first at all. On the possibility of alteration, i.e. of arising and ceasing in general.",
    "reflm:3935": "The propositions: all occurrences have consequences (each one), and all together in turn have consequences, are not the same. All rational synthetic propositions are subjective, and conversely only the analytic ones are objective. Principles of identity and contradiction (principia identitatis et contradictionis). The synthesis of reason (g rational) or of experience (g empirical). The first is either of coordination: whole and parts, number and unity, or of subordination: ground and consequence. [Empirisch] The second, of coordination according to space and time.",
    "reflm:3942": "True is a cognition that agrees with the constitution of the object. Because the representation of outer objects is possible only through the idea of space, all axioms of space, and what is derived from them, agree with the object, as do all relations of concepts according to the rule of identity (Regel der Identität). For the ideas then agree with one another. But since the metaphysical concepts of ground, substance, etc. are not properly representations of objects, inasmuch as even the most perfect sense can sense these in no thing, and things could all be represented without these relations, albeit not by our reason: these concepts are not objective; hence in their axioms everything is subjective. Therefore, if they are falsely regarded (g as) objective, neither truth nor falsity holds of them. In general, if one wants to find the concept of cause, one will find, apart from the relations of connection according to ideas of time, no explanation that would not include a circle, and it seems to be nothing else. The surest proof that they are not objective is that they stand in evident contradiction.",
    "reflm:3948": "If the question were whether new discoveries are still to be hoped for in the empirical sciences, there is no doubt of it, because more experiences yield more and new cognitions. If the question is whether in the science of pure reason [mehr] new discoveries are to be expected, then it is clear that in mathematics, logic and pure morals many are possible, because according to the rule of identity (Regel der Identität) consequences [werden] can be inferred from universal concepts according to all sorts of arbitrarily given combinations. But with metaphysics it is quite different. Here the question is not what flows from the universal to the particular, according to the rule of identity, in arbitrarily assumed relations, but which relations actually lie at the first ground of universal rules. Now because these relations are given neither through the senses in experience nor through the understanding in an intuitive and singular concept, nothing more can be done than to analyse one's confused ideas. From this, however, only a science of the subject can arise. Since no object is given here, we would also be unable, through this cutting up and analysing of our ideas, to discover anything about it.",
    "reflm:3950": "Synthetic [Sätze] concepts are space, time and number. [und sind obiectiv.] Even if the concepts are synthetic, but the propositions are analytic, i.e. according to the rule of identity (Regel der Identität), then they are objective. But if concepts are subjective, i.e. of no object at all, neither of a given condition of the representation of objects — space and time — nor [von] arbitrary concepts of quantity, then the judgments are nevertheless objective and universally valid, i.e. either universally true or false, [sind aber] if their form is analytic. But if [alsdenn] their form is synthetic, then they are subjective, and they hold objectively only as rules of appearances, hence as empirical judgments.",
    "reflm:3952": "Metaphysics is a science of the laws of pure human reason, and therefore subjective. Objective pure philosophy has either analytic principles without any axioms of experience, or synthetic ones. The former [besteht] rests on universal judgments according to the rule of identity (Regel der Identität) and on the subordination of the particular under the universal. Hence the universally valid rules of the understanding and of the will, consequently pure logic and morals (Logica et Moralis pura). The second has as its object the most universal of outer and inner sense, and therefore contains the pure grounds of reason to which all natural sciences of outer and inner sense can be brought.",
    "reflm:3975": "301. Because through reason, i.e. through the cognition that is not sensation, only concepts arise, through which it remains undetermined whether the thing is posited or not, something is required in addition that necessitates us to posit something. Hence the concept of a ground has a subjective origin, and the expression: something is posited through another thing or for the sake of another, means objectively only: things accompany one another or follow upon one another. The idea of ground (g cause) arises from experiences. To cognize through reason that something is not, or that something is not possible, is one and the same; that something is, or that something is necessary, is one and the same. On the other hand, everything appears as contingent that I cognize through the senses, but not through reason, to be. The only concepts of pure reason through whose relation truths can be discovered according to the rule of identity (Regel der Identität) are magnitudes; but these too are arbitrary fictions.",
    "reflm:3991": "Through the principle of contradiction (principium contradictionis) it is not the possibility of things that is cognized, but that of their predicates or of their relation.",
    "reflm:4004": "Every concept is possible in which there lies a predicate that is considered in the subject and does not contradict it; but not every synthesis in which there is no contradiction is possible. I.e. real relations are not understood through the principle of contradiction (Satz des Widerspruchs).",
    "reflm:4005": "Through reason we can cognize only conditioned possibility. The absolute possibility that we cognize through reason comes about according to the principle of contradiction (principium contradictionis) and concerns propositions into which we resolve the concepts. All absolute possibility must otherwise be given through experience; what is, is in itself possible.",
    "reflm:4012": "The principle of reason (principium rationis), as also the principle of the consequence (principium rationati), is a rule of sound reason and is therefore restricted to the objects of experience. The limits of the sensible world are thus also the limits of its use. If one goes further and wants to make its validity universal, one errs and brings it into science.",
    "reflm:4035": "All necessity is a necessity of judgments or of things; in the former, when I want to think the opposite, I retain the subject and cancel its identical predicate; in the latter, when I want to think the opposite, I cancel the thing with all its predicates; hence in the former case I can cognize the (g absolute) necessity from the principle of contradiction (Satz des Widerspruchs), but not in the latter.",
    "reflm:4049": "Consequences are also connected with grounds, but not as parts [mit] in a whole. The connection of the parts with the whole is [zu] according to the rule of identity (Regel der Identität), but that of the parts among one another is not according to it. Often one has first the idea of the whole or of divisibility (dispertibilitas); e.g. a universal concept with respect to the classes, space with respect to the parts. A whole is ideal or real (totum ideale aut reale); the former either logical or arbitrary (prius vel logicum vel arbitrarium).",
    "reflm:4275": "228. 432. 1380. Analysis of reason. Principle of contradiction (principium contradictionis), of identity (identitatis); yields objectively valid propositions. Synthesis of reason: various laws (axiomata subreptitia, surreptitious axioms), subjectively valid propositions. The conditions of our reason, which 1. cognizes objects only mediately and not through intuition, hence the conditions by means of which a cognition of something is possible for it, and the necessity of positing something primitively and without conditions. Intuitions of the senses (g according to sensible form and matter) yield synthetic propositions that are objective. Crusius explains the real principles of reason [vor] according to the system of preformation (systema praeformationis) (g from subjective principles), Locke according to physical influx (influxus physicus) like Aristotle, Plato and Malebranche from intellectual intuition (intuitus intellectualis), we according to epigenesis from the use of the natural laws of reason. (g Common human understanding, the sense of the true and false (sensus veri et falsi), is an occult quality (qualitas occulta).) Antithesis: a method of reason for discovering the opposition of the subjective laws, which, if by the fault of subreption (vitium subreptionis) it is taken for objective, is scepticism (in the objective sense); but if it is only a [critic des subiects] propaedeutic, then it is the sceptical method (methodus sceptica) for determining the subjective laws of reason. Subjective antithesis.",
    "reflm:4290": "Whether one cannot invent something through reason according to analogy. Natural theology, because all realities do not contradict one another. The question is why magnitudes are the only thing of which the human understanding makes concepts out of itself, whose possibilities it understands by itself, and in whose relations it can arrive, through the principle of contradiction (Satz des Widerspruchs), at certainty and at infinite extension.",
    "reflm:4300": "A and not-A are contradictorily opposed; A and B are disparate. Between contradictory opposites there is no third (non datur tertium) (which is neither A nor not-A).",
    "reflm:4303": "On M § 54: The principle of contradiction (principium contradictionis) is the formal condition, but not the principle, of all possibility.",
    "reflm:4334": "On freedom. We can regard an action of ours either as something that happens, i.e. as appearance, or as something that ought to happen, i.e. as an intuition of self-activity towards possible effects. In the first case it is [an] subject to the laws of determining grounds; in the second case it is an intellectual one, in which the subject is not passive; here the rule of the determining ground (regula rationis determinantis) governs only as a principle [cognosc] of comprehension or a logical principle (principium comprehensionis oder logicum), i.e. that one cannot cognize it a priori (except in the case of a perfect understanding) otherwise than through determining grounds. The good understanding has determining grounds, but according to laws of the understanding through intellectual motives (motiva); the affected one surrenders itself to sensibility. Something happens necessarily, i.e. according to the empirical concept of necessity, which nevertheless admits a contingency according to concepts of reason. The understanding must itself excite sensibility, so that it may determine the action; hence it happens according to laws of sensibility and yet of the understanding. We also know only the proximate determining grounds of sensibility, and according to these the action must always be capable of being explained.",
    "reflm:4370": "How do I know that canons of reason are not axioms, i.e. can be used only a posteriori and descendendo (descending), but not a priori and ascendendo (ascending)? For the subjective laws of reason are rules of its use in application, but the objective ones in explanation. All immediately certain propositions are either 1. fundamental formulae or 2. axioms or 3. canons or 4. elementary propositions of analysis or 5. immediately certain propositions of synthesis. The first are the principle of identity (Satz der Identität) and of contradiction (des Widerspruchs). The second: [Nur grund] objective principles of synthesis, space and time. The third: objective principles of qualitative synthesis (synthesis qualitativa). The 4th and fifth: material propositions contained immediately under the principles of form [und der] of synthesis as well as of analysis. [Axiomata] Primitive formulae (formulae primitivae) hold for every cognition. The synthetic fundamental concepts of reason, just like the synthetic fundamental concepts of appearances, give occasion to axioms, both of which, however, serve only for the use of reason a posteriori. The use of principles a posteriori, when one applies them a priori, is a rational cognition of objects by analogy (per analogiam), [entweder] e.g. of space, of pleasure and displeasure, from God.",
    "reflm:4460": "[Analytischer Theil Princ] Genetic part: sensibility and reason. Zetetic part: a. Analysis, principle of contradiction (Principium Contradictionis). b. Synthesis. c. Antithesis. Sceptical part.",
    "reflm:4477": "Analytic propositions can be proved from the principle of contradiction (principium contradictionis) or of identity (identitatis), but not synthetic ones; whence, then, do we arrive at these? 1. Empirically. 2. Through pure intuition. 3. Through subjective conditions of the representation of the understanding.",
    "reflm:4478": "The principle of all synthetic judgments of pure reason (not of pure intuition) is: everything that contains the conditions without which an apprehension would be impossible is true. The principle of contradiction (principium contradictionis) holds of all cognitions insofar as they are regarded [auf] as merely possible. I.e. what contradicts the concept that I could have of an object is false.",
    "reflm:4480": "No predicate belongs to a subject which contradicts an analytic predicate of the subject. No predicate which contradicts a synthetic predicate of a subject belongs to it at the same time as that predicate. With respect to synthetic predicates, therefore, every thing is alterable, and synthesis can be thought as successive determination. The possibility of concepts rests solely on the principle of contradiction (Satz des Widerspruchs). That of synthesis, on experience.",
    "reflm:4483": "In the outer margin, beginning beside the 3rd sentence of M § 18: Even impossibility requires something given. The first data are not cognized a priori as possible, but constitute the condition of all our judgments about possibility, so that only that is possible which agrees with the conditions [der] a priori of empirical cognition. Logical possibility, the principle of contradiction (principium contradictionis), is not objective, but of cognition. We can devise no possibility of intuition, of reality, of real relation, of the necessary, except insofar as [es] the principles of these have been given in experience.",
    "reflm:4570": "All our possibility presupposes data, [and these] either for all things with the indeterminacy of each, or a given principle (principium) of relation with the indeterminacy of a particular one, e.g. in space triangles are possible. The logical relation is the principle of contradiction (Satz des Wiederspruchs); that of phenomena (phaenomenorum) is space and time; those of things and their real relations are forces. From the former one cannot infer to the latter. The principle of contradiction is usable only negatively, namely for cognizing the impossible through contradiction; but positively, i.e. for the possible, it presupposes too much, although in this way too it is true.",
    "reflm:4634": "940. M XXII: We know every object only through predicates which we say or think of it. Prior to that, whatever is found of representations in us is to be counted only as materials, but not as cognition. Hence an object is only a something in general, which we think to ourselves through certain predicates that make up its concept. In every judgment there are accordingly two predicates which we compare with one another. Of these, the one [through which we the] which makes up the given cognition of the object is called the logical subject; the second, which is compared with it, is called the logical predicate. When I say: a body is divisible, this means as much as: something [which] x, which I know under the predicates that together make up a concept of body, I also think through the predicate of divisibility. x is the same as xb. Now a as well as b belongs to x. But M XXIII: in different ways: either in such a way that b already lies in that which makes up the concept a, and can thus be found through analysis of it, or in such a way that b belongs to the x without being contained and comprehended in a. In the first case the judgment is analytic, in the second synthetic. The case cited is that of an analytic judgment; but the proposition: every body is heavy, is a synthesis; the one predicate is not involved in the subject but added to it. Now all analytic judgments can be seen a priori; and what can be cognized only a posteriori is synthetic. Hence judgments that are properly empirical are synthetic. But there are nevertheless judgments [which a] whose validity seems to be established a priori which are nonetheless synthetic, e.g. everything alterable has a cause; how does one arrive at these judgments? [through which ha] whence do we get the right to join to one concept another of the very same object, which no observation and experience shows in it? Nonetheless all axioms properly so called are such synthetic propositions, e.g. between two points there can be only one straight line. On the other hand the proposition: [Every magnitude is itself If one Every part is smal] Every magnitude is equal to itself, is an analytic proposition. The principle (Principium) or norm of all analytic propositions is the principle of contradiction (Satz des Wiederspruchs) and of identity (der identitaet). It is (if I take the two together) no axiom, but a formula, i.e. a universal model of analytic propositions; for it contains no middle term (medium terminum). We therefore have judgments a posteriori which are synthetic, but also judgments a priori which are nevertheless synthetic and for that reason cannot be derived from any experience, because they contain a true universality, hence necessity, and also comprise nothing but concepts which could not have been drawn from experience. [Whence] These concepts may reside in us from wherever they like: whence do we get this connection of them? Are they revelations, prejudices, etc.? If certain concepts in us contain nothing other than that through which all experiences are possible on our side, then they can be asserted a priori, prior to experience and yet with complete validity for everything that may ever come before us. They then hold, to be sure, not of things in general, but yet of everything that can ever be given to us through experience, because they contain the conditions through which these experiences are possible. Such propositions will therefore contain the condition of the possibility not of things but of experience. M XXIV: But things that cannot be given to us through any experience are nothing for us; hence we can very well use such propositions as universal for a practical purpose, only not as principles of speculation about objects in general. Now in order to determine what sort of concepts these are which must necessarily precede all experience and through which alone it is possible, which are therefore given a priori and also contain the ground of judgments a priori, we must analyze an experience in general. In every experience there is something through which an object is given to us, and something through which it is thought. If we take the conditions which lie in the activities of the mind through which alone it can be given, then one can cognize something of the objects a priori. If we take that through which alone it can be thought, then one can also cognize something a priori of all possible objects. For only thereby does something become an object for us, or a cognition of it. We shall investigate the former. That through which an object (g of experience) is given to us is called appearance. The possibility of appearances, on the side of the human mind, is sensibility. In sensibility there is a matter, which is called sensation, and with respect to it and its diversity we are merely passive, and the manifoldness of impressions means that we find nothing a priori in us which we might know a priori from ourselves prior to the impressions. One can never represent to oneself in thought any [new] impression of a new kind. But appearances also have a form, a ground lying in our subject, through which we either order the impressions themselves or that which corresponds to them, and give each part of them its place. This [is] can be nothing other than an activity which, though naturally excited by the impressions, can nevertheless be cognized by itself. (When we posit something in space and in time, we act; when we posit it alongside and after one another, we connect. These actions are only means for bringing about those places; but one can take them separately; when we posit the same thing several times, or in the one action at the same time posit the other, then this is a kind of actions through which we posit something in accordance with the rule of appearances, whereby this positing must have its particular rules, which are distinct from the conditions of the form, how they are to be placed with respect to appearance.)",
    "reflm:4654": "In and The contrapositum of the principle of contradiction (Satz des Wiederspruchs) is a subjective law. On thinkability (Denklichkeit), the supreme criterion of truth (criterio veritatis) of Crusius.",
    "reflm:4655": "In and The principle of identity (principium identitatis) and of contradiction (contradictionis) are both called [principle] of contradiction (contradictionis). Indemonstrable judgments stand immediately under it; material principles, in which the predicate is determined, are indemonstrable. Is the principle of judgments and of their possibility. The possibility of things requires a real ground.",
    "reflm:4676": "S. I-II: (g In all reality there is the relation of substance to accident; in what happens: of ground to consequence, etc.) The principle of identity and of contradiction (Satz der identitaet und Wiederspruchs) contains the comparison of two predicates a and b with x, but only [through] in such a way that [x by mea] the concept a of x is compared with b (substantive), so that the x becomes superfluous. It is a principle of form, not of content, hence merely logical. A principle of analysis, from which nothing is cognized objectively. [It can In cat] It can be cognized in categorical, hypothetical and disjunctive form. If I refer both predicates to the x and thereby to one another, then it is synthetic: no x who is learned is without knowledge (Wissenschaft), for here [it means] the restriction of time is needed, namely: at the same time. The (g lack of) knowledge contradicts learnedness, to be sure, but not the human being x who is learned, except insofar as he is learned. Thus the contradiction is directed either at the concept a which I have of x, or at x, to which this concept does not necessarily attach. The synthetic validity of b and non b with respect to the x, which can be thought through the concept a or non a, is called change (wechsel). But if a cannot be separated from b in x, e.g. no x which is a body is indivisible, then it is to be seen that the x which is thought through a could never be thought through non a, that no [body] being which has the nature of a body could ever become incorporeal, and that the a in itself is no predicate with respect to the x, but is a reciprocal concept (wechselbegrif) with it and thus valid substantive. Difference between affirmative and negative propositions and the principles of affirmation and negation, which are equal as to their contents. –––––––––– But if a and b are not identical, whether they be used affirmatively or negatively, and x is not wholly (g determined) thought through the concept of a, then a and b stand not in a logical but in a real relation (g something different) of combination, hence not of [identit] involution. Thus their relation is determined not through [them] their concepts in themselves, but by means of the x, of which a contains the designation. How are such syntheses possible? x must be a datum of sensibility [which then through] in which a synthesis, i.e. a relation of coordination, takes place; for [a discursive] this contains more than is thought through its concept a, and is the representation of a in concreto. Now there are three cases where a transcendental subject is sensible and [through conc] furnishes a relation of concepts: either that it is the [construction] intuition of a, or the appearance of a, or the appearance of a (g or the empirical cognition). In the first case the relation of a:b [is] follows from the construction of the a=x. In the second [and third] from [the R Exp] [example of the] [empirical object of a in th] the sensible condition of the intellection of a; in the third drawn from observation. The first two syntheses are a priori (all three objective). Because in the second case a [the an experiential c] signifies a universally sensible condition of perception, but x the condition of the subject in general, in which the relation of all perceptions is determinable (for perceptions are not merely objective but presuppose sensation, which has only subjective validity). Thus [the Re] a will signify the universal of perception, x the (g sensible) condition of the subject [in which] (substratum), in which this perception is to receive its place. Consequently the condition of disposition. b, finally, the universal function of the mind to determine for a its place in x, hence the exponent of the relation of perceptions [in the mind to one another], and consequently to determine their place according to a rule. S. III: When something is apprehended, it is taken up into the function of apperception. I am, I think, thoughts are in me. These are all relations which, to be sure, do not give rules of appearance, but bring it about that all appearance is represented as contained under rules. The I makes up the substratum for a rule in general, and apprehension relates every appearance to it. For a rule to arise three pieces are required: 1. x. as the datum for a rule (object of sensibility, or rather sensible real representation). 2. a. the aptitude (aptitudo) for the rule, or the condition through which it is related to a rule at all. 3. b. the exponent of the rule. Now if a norm is to arise for the rule of appearances in general, or of experiences, e.g. Everything existing is in substance, then x is sensation in general as the specif: of reality. By being represented as reality, it becomes the matter of a rule, or [it] sensation becomes capable of a rule, and a is only a function of the apprehension of the appearance as given in general. Now because everything must be given in time, and time thus comprises everything within itself, b. is an [function] act (actus) of apperception, namely [the] the consciousness of the subject which apperceives, as of that which is given in the whole of time, is necessarily bound up with it, for otherwise the sensation would not be represented as belonging to me. –––––––––– Transcendental thetic: of the establishment of the principles of pure reason. Antithetic: of the (g natural) use of these principles [in general]. For general logic also deals with the natural use [of the same] in the common understanding. The basic rules of the latter are abstracted from the common understanding, though not borrowed and derived from it. But the universal rules or principles of thinking in general without determinate objects, or the determination of cognition from the relation to objects, is always dialectical. S. IV: We think everything to ourselves through predicates, hence there is always a relation to x. In judgments, however, there is a relation of a:b, both of which refer to x. a and b in x, x by means of a:b, finally a+b=x. –––––––––– The (g absolute) predicate in general is reality and of what. Determinate predicates (g relational predicates) which are real pertain only to relations. Of these there are three, according to the three relations in judging. The relational predicates are transcendental; the relation of predicates is logical. What action in the mind does the relational predicate express, and on what is its reference grounded, on the one side to sensibility, on the other side to the logical, so that through the first it receives reality, through the second the form of thinking? Is the x the form of (g inner) sensibility or the real of apprehension?",
    "reflm:4679": "S. I: We are conscious of ourselves and of our own actions, and of appearances insofar as we become conscious of the apprehension of them, either by coordinating them with one another or by apprehending one sensation through another. We would thus not become conscious of appearances at all if we did not bricht ab. An object of the senses is only that which acts on my senses, hence acts and is thus substance. Hence the category of substance is principial. Every beginning of a state of representation is always a transition from a previous one, for otherwise we would not perceive that the former had begun. Hence, since the same subject always holds of the one object as well as of the other, and the boundary is also common to both, the following belongs to the previous one as to that which determines it. In the unity of the mind a whole is possible only through the mind's reciprocally determining one partial representation from another, and all of them together being comprehended in one action which holds of all. –––––––––– x:a = m:n If a indicates the condition through which x is given, then the relation of a:b follows according to the principle of contradiction (Satz des Wiederspruchs) and holds universally without the restriction of the at the same time. E.g. in every reality (it is given only through the action of the subject) there is a relation of substance to accident. x here signifies the subject, a the apprehension of the object. x:a is thus the relation of the original action of the bricht ab. –––––––––– Mere apprehension already explains that behind the appearance there must be a substance, a cause of the composition; but observation and judgment must show which is the substance etc. Where there is an action, there is substance, e.g. with light, warmth; but whether light is a substance does not flow from apprehension but from the exposition of the appearance. That something is represented as having happened is enough to regard it as an effect; for the apprehension of it is in fact an effect which occurs in the mind etc. The intellectual functions thus make the beginning in apprehension, but specification gives us the rule of application of this concept; hence determinate rules of synthesis can be given only through experience, but the universal norm of them a priori. S. II: Empirical intuition is appearance. Appearance of which one is conscious is perception. Every perception must be brought under a title of the understanding, because otherwise it yields no concept at all and nothing is thought in it. By means of these concepts we make use of appearances, or rather the concepts indicate the manner in which we make use of appearances as the matter for thinking. 1. of intuition in general for magnitude, 2. of sensation, in order to determine the real relation in appearance. We say: the stone weighs, the wood falls (g the body moves), i.e. it acts, hence it is substance. The field is prepared, the meadow dried out, the glass broken: these are effects which relate to a cause. The wall is solid, the wax soft, the gold dense: these are connections in the composite. Without such concepts the appearances would all be separated and would not belong to one another. If they have equal relations to one another in space or time, these are nevertheless not determined from the objects of the appearances, but only placed alongside one another. Experience is an understood perception. But we understand it when we represent it to ourselves under titles of the understanding. Experience is a specification of the concepts of the understanding through given appearances. Appearances are the matter or the substrate. Experiences are thus possible only through its being presupposed that all appearances belong under titles of the understanding, i.e. in all mere intuition there is magnitude, in all appearance substance and accident; in the change of them cause and effect, in the whole of them interaction. Thus these propositions hold of all objects of experience. These same propositions also hold of the mind with respect to the generation of its own representations, and are moments of the genesis. But all appearances must be brought under the titles of apperception, so that they, as regards intuition, both constructed bricht ab. But the conditions of subsumption under these concepts are taken from the sensible relation, which stands in analogy with the action of the understanding and belongs to inner sense, of which apperception the bricht ab. Whence is it that what acts is regarded as if it were permanent, and as if only the actions, effects and compositions varied?",
    "reflm:4680": "Everything that happens is, for the sake of the determination of its concept among appearances, i.e. with a view to the possibility of experience, represented as contained under a rule, the relation of which is expressed through a concept of the understanding. In the appearance x, therefore, in which a is a concept, there must be contained, besides what is thought through a, conditions of its specification which make necessary a rule whose function is expressed through b. a cannot be specifically determined in time, when it happens, otherwise than by means of a rule. Thus no experience of a can take place without a rule. Thus the principle of sufficient reason (Satz des zureichenden Grundes) is a principle (principium) of the rule of experience, namely of instituting it. The proposition that everything which follows upon something in time follows upon something else in accordance with a rule, or that with respect to its succession a rule obtains, does not lie in the specification of the concept a of happening or of contingency, for there only the appearance is meant. (But happening is already an existence according to a rule of time.) The ordering of appearances according to relations of space and time requires a rule, just as appearance itself requires a form.",
    "reflm:4713": "93'. E II 860. 861. 894. Everything that exists is necessary, either absolutely or conditionally*, thus as the effect of another (ut causatum alterius). *(s We therefore also cognize in all relations of the manifold in the composite a necessity which must always be derived. Nothing can be originally contingent (g M 93': rather the reverse: every effect (causatum) is contingent in itself). M 93: The only question is: what is contingent? and on what does the principle of necessity (principium necessitatis) rest? On this: that otherwise there would be no determining grounds a priori.) The principle of the consequence (principium rationati) says only: everything that happens is necessary under a condition. Everything must be objectively determined a priori. Everything alterable has a beginning, thus comes into being and is contingent and is the effect of another (causatum alterius).",
    "reflm:4723": "Appearances are representations insofar as we are affected. The representation of our (g free) self-activity is one in which we are not affected; consequently it is not appearance, but [orig free] apperception. Now the principle of sufficient reason (Satz des Zureichenden Grundes) holds only as a principle (principium) of the exposition of appearances, consequently not as exposition of the original intuitions.",
    "reflm:4729": "Negations in general mean: some realities not. But this presupposes the opposite, namely all with a limitation. Thus the concept of all reality is necessary to us as a substratum of reason; but we cannot for that reason regard a highest reality as necessary in itself. Further, according to the principle of excluded middle (principio exclusi medii), every thing is considered in relation to everything possible, as in a division, hence in a whole of reality. The existence of a thing can never be proved from mere concepts, because existence is not one of the predicates, and because from concepts nothing further can be inferred than the respective affirming or denying, but not the absolute positing of the object with its predicates. The concept which lies at the ground is a necessary presupposition and therefore seems to be a concept of the necessary being.",
    "reflm:4759": "S. I: On the principles of empirical [exposition] intellection, as distinguished from the principles of comprehension. How one can deceive both the dogmatic enemy of religion and the dogmatic brooder over religion through a seeming antithetic and thus a feigned scepticism. How one can refute this scepticism itself through the dogmatic principles [of the positive] with respect to the practical use of reason. How one can derive from the principles of the unity of reason with respect to totality principles which conflict with the [conditions] principles of determination in appearances, which must always be partial and conditioned, but which must be regarded as belonging to cognition in the whole. There is a prototypal and an ectypal synthesis (synthesin prototypam und ectypam): the former of self-determination from an [a priori] a priori terminus, not empirically given (a termino a priori, non empirice dato), from nothing; the latter from an a posteriori terminus (a termino a posteriori), — the former absolutely (simpliciter), the latter in a certain respect (secundum qvid). I am, I act, I together with all the manifold, I insofar as I exclude the manifold. The principles of [s] absolute synthesis are rational and conditions of practical cognition a priori. –––––––––– 1. Principles of intuition. [Of] That conditions of intuition are not [conditions] of things. 2. Of the understanding, e.g. the principle of reason (principium rationis). 3. Of reason. –––––––––– Reason goes from the universal to the particular [or from], the understanding from the particular to the universal. The latter universal is such only in a certain respect (secundum qvid) and belongs to the empirical or physical use of reason. The former is absolute and belongs to the free or metaphysical use. Likewise to the moral. S. II: (g Exposition and [comprehension] rationality.) (g To appearances.) (g Unity of experience and unity of reason.) Principles of the exposition of appearances presuppose [them] these all as conditioned, hence nothing posited absolutely. 1. no absolute totality (g totality in a certain respect, totalitas secundum qvid) [of synthesis] of composition, hence the [regress infin] progress infinite. (g unconditioned limitation in the phenomenal world (in mundo phaenomeno). Among phenomena there is no absolute universality (Inter phaenomena non datur universitas absoluta).) 2. no absolute [division of the] totality of decomposition, hence nothing unconditionedly simple. (g no absolute simplicity, non simplicitas absoluta.) (g The infinite progression cannot be comprehended, and the unconditioned cannot be made intuitive.) 3. no absolute totality of the series of generation, no unconditioned spontaneity. (g no absolute causality, non causalitas absoluta.) 4. no unconditioned necessity. All things can be removed from time and space. (g no absolute necessity, non necessitas absoluta.) (g World in the physical sense Simple — — — spontaneity (spontaneitas) — — necessity (necessitas) — —) All these propositions are (g objectively) certain as principles of the empirical use, but contrary to reason. (g To things in general.) Principles of rationality or comprehension (g of them). From the universal to the particular: absolute synthesis. 1. Unconditioned All of the (g dependent) whole. Origin of the world. (g in the noumenal world there is universality, in mundo noumeno datur universitas.) 2. The unconditionedly simple. (g monad, monas.) 3. Unconditioned spontaneity of action. (g transcendental freedom, libertas transscendentalis.) 4. Unconditionedly necessary existence. (g absolute original necessity, necessitas absoluta originaria.) (g World in the metaphysical sense Simple — — — —) These propositions are subjectively necessary as principles of the use of reason in the whole of cognition: unity of the whole of the manifold of the cognition of the understanding. They are practically necessary with respect to the bricht ab. Subjective conflict between the partiality of sensibility and the totality of reason in the determination of cognitions: of the conditions of the empirical use in the exposition of appearances, of the conditions of the rational use in the comprehension of appearances. [The empirical canons] The canon of the empirical use pertains to universality (universalitaet) (g of appearances), that of the rational use to the universality as totality (universitatem) of things. What cannot be determined in appearance is not for that reason impossible. There must be principles of the self-determination of reason which differ from those in which reason is determined through appearances and their conditions. These are principles of the unity of cognitions in the whole, hence not of partial but of total unity.",
    "reflm:4854": "The first principle of concepts is being (ens); that of judgments is the principle of contradiction (principium contradictionis).",
    "reflm:4872": "M XII: All analytic judgments have as their ground the principle of identity (Satz der identitaet) and of contradiction (contradiction). The synthetic ones have as many axioms as there are synthetic concepts (conceptus synthetici) given through the nature of the understanding (per intellectus naturam). These synthetic concepts are the grounds by which the understanding can attain empirical concepts of things [If], and so the synthetic principles (principia synthetica) hold only with regard to the laws of experience, hence a posteriori, and are practically true. But in abstracto they are only subjectively valid. M XI: (g judgments of experience)",
    "reflm:4893": "1135. Locke an influxionist, at the same time a physiologist of the understanding. Lambert an analyst and architectonic. Wolff a mere dogmatist and a mathematical head. Crusius a preestablishist (praestabilist) of reason. (g denied the subordination of all principles under the principle of contradiction (principium contradictionis) and yet wanted to give an origin; can give no mark by which to tell which principles are implanted and which are foisted in.) Empiricists of pure reason. Sound understanding. Misologists. Our purpose of metaphysics. (In the senses there is nothing true (In sensibus nihil esse veri).) Value. 1. Distinction of phenomena and noumena.* 2. Origin of the latter. Innate, mystical; or acquired, logical. Plato, Leibniz. Aristotle, Locke. Formulae abstracted from the senses or not. *(g distinction of the objects or of the cognition of them.)",
    "reflm:5043": "The propositions that are seen immediately after the principle of contradiction (Satz des Wiederspruchs) are not seen from it. For this principle, as to its entire validity, can after all be cognized convincingly only in concreto.",
    "reflm:5137": "Über und The principle of contradiction (principium contradictionis) is the negative universal principle, but not the only one.",
    "reflm:5138": "Principle of contradiction (principium contradictionis): the restricting, not constitutive, principle of all cognition, a condition without which not (conditio sine qva non).",
    "reflm:5139": "The principle of contradiction (principium contradictionis) is the restricting, not the constitutive, principle.",
    "reflm:5154": "The principle of determination (principium determinationis). Is distinct from the principle of thoroughgoing determination (principium omnimodae determinationis).",
    "reflm:5164": "5. E II 1369. M 5': Between two logically opposed things (logice oppositis) there is no third, but between really opposed things (realiter oppositis) (willingly, unwillingly) there is indifference =0*. Motion and rest are not contradictory opposites (contradictorie opposita). For neither of them agrees with God and the world-whole. Such opposites are both restricted by a condition, as here that of existence in space; if the condition falls away, there is no contradiction. E.g. the actions of human beings are, as appearances, M 5: not free, but as intellectualia free. The one proposition here has one condition, the other an opposite one; in this case the opposite of the predicate (oppositum praedicati) can also be asserted of the other, and so two apparent opposites can be true at the same time. There is a necessary being in the ascending series (in serie ascendente), but not in the descending. In the one case it is a hypothesis of reason (g on the presupposition of the members); in the other no such hypothesis is possible, for the former is conditioned, the latter categorical. M 5': *(s The first can be seen, the second not.)",
    "reflm:5166": "In und The logical concept of possibility: the principle of contradiction (principium contradictionis) (g analysis), or the real concept: principles of synthesis (principia der synthesis).",
    "reflm:5167": "5. E II 505. Subjectively, representations A and B can follow one another thus or also the other way round. In order that the sequence hold objectively, i.e. serve for experience, it must be determined, so that I cannot reverse it. Why is the principle of sufficient reason (principium rationis sufficientis) posited, next to the principle of contradiction (principio contradictionis), as the only one? The principle of M 5: determination, derivation and division.",
    "reflm:5176": "Besides the principle of identity and of contradiction (principio identitatis et contradictionis) there must be still other principles of connection (nexus) and of opposition. For through the former only logical connection and opposition (nexus und oppositio logica) can be seen, but not real. Which, then, are these synthetic principles (principia synthetica)?",
    "reflm:5178": "In und The possibility of a concept from the principle of contradiction (Satz der contradictio); the possibility of the thing, not.",
    "reflm:5189": "7. E II 1072. The principle of reason (principium rationis) as anticipation, hence with the conditions of sensibility, is objective. (g for in time there is always something preceding.*) The principle of reason as a postulate of insight (perspicientz) is subjective. The principle of termination (principium terminationis) as a demand of comprehension is subjective. If both are made objective, they conflict with each other; subjectively, however, not. M 7: *(g What, as contingent (arising, transitory (transitorium)), is given only [through] by means of sensation and not a priori by [itself] ourselves, has a ground. (s but contingent means precisely that which exists not for itself but only as a consequence (rationatum).) Free actions are given a priori. Events, however, or what comes to pass, only through experience and a posteriori. The former is the beginning (terminus), to be cognized according to a universal law; the second cannot be cognized according to universal rules without an antecedent (antecedens), and for that requires a ground.)",
    "reflm:5190": "The principle of reason in the world (Principium rationis in mundo) (g is cosmological, not transcendental) is a principle of cognizing, not of acting (principium cognoscendi, non agendi).",
    "reflm:5202": "7'. E II 1085. 1084. The principle of reason (principium rationis) is the principle of the determination of things in temporal succession; for this cannot be determined through time, but time must be determined through the rule of the existence of appearances in the understanding. Principle of the possibility of experience. M 7': Thus it is not possible to determine for things their place in time without presupposing this principle, by which the course of appearances is first of all made uniform. Succession in the concept of consequence (Folge) concerns only the sequence [of that] in positing.",
    "reflm:5207": "The question whether the principle of reason (principium rationis) is subjective or objective, and in what sense it is objective, is grounded on the discussion of the question: by what do I cognize empirically that something is a ground and something a consequence? Without doubt only by this, that A can never be without B, but B can well be without A. Now that everything is, as it were, in one series, and indeed where each thing is connected with an antecedent (antecedens) according to a universal rule of order, that is the ground — —",
    "reflm:5209": "Since we do not see the possibility of a real ground: how then are we to say a priori that there must be such grounds throughout, except where something, according to the rule of identity (Regel der identitaet), already refers to a presupposition? Does this principle not hold as anticipation, because without a rule we would also have no experiences, but this rule consists in the order of time and of space according to universal laws?",
    "reflm:5220": "The principle of sufficient reason (Satz des zureichenden Grundes) is the principle of order in the course of nature; that of freedom, the principle of the beginning in the series of things. Because there must be a beginning in the series of consequences, there must be freedom, and even within the course first beginnings are possible. If there is nothing but nature, then the series of the [subordi] connected is continuous. But if there is freedom, it is interrupted.",
    "reflm:5263": "1011. 974. 1142. [What contradicts itself is not in accordance with the conditions of understanding.] How the conflict of the subjective conditions, or their presupposition, imitates and substitutes for the truth of the objective ones. E.g. a mathematically infinite is possible because it does not conflict with the rules of insight; it is impossible because it conflicts with the conditions of comprehension. Everything has its ground, because without this the being of the thing cannot be seen; something has no further ground above itself, because otherwise it would conflict with the conditions of comprehending. There is no freedom (transcendental). There is one. There is an absolutely necessary being, because without this there is no comprehending. There is no etc. being, because the same conflicts with insight. But if these concepts are taken only according to the conditions of apprehension, then they are restricted to the objects of the senses and also have only a restricted meaning. E.g. everything that happens has, in a certain order, a first ground; or rather, the axioms are not transcendent but hold only as anticipations. So that they hold only of the data of the senses, insofar as these can be understood. E.g. what happens has a ground, because without a rule according to which it is given according to laws of sensibility, one would think no object through the representation. The principle of contradiction (principium contradictionis) contains the conditions (conditiones) of thinking in general. The anticipations, which affirm the conditions of apprehension of the concepts of the understanding (e.g. in every substance there is something enduring (aliqvid perdurabile), or a substance always endures), contain the conditions (g postulates) of understanding and are therefore always true with regard to the sensible conditions. (To understand something under its determinations (representations) means: to think or posit an object that corresponds to them.) Apprehension without the conditions under which alone something can be given is a function, but not yet a concept, or rather potential. To understand something is: to cognize it, since it is given beforehand; but to have insight (einsehen) is: to cognize a priori; to understand logically is: to cognize something distinctly.",
    "reflm:5270": "42'. E II 1623. The principle of thoroughgoing determination (das princip der durchgängigen Bestimmung): whatever exists is thoroughly determined (qvodlibet existens est omnimode determinatum), i.e. any being whatever can in itself be given only as thoroughly determined, but through the concept of it it can be undetermined in many ways. That proposition has in thought the sum total of all possible predicates with their opposites and, since to existence belongs only reality, the sum total of all realities with their opposites; and, since every thing that does not contain all reality always presupposes another that contains it, it also has before its eyes the concept of a thing that contains all reality, as of a logically original being (entis logice originarii), whose reality, or the consequences of it, through limitation yield all things, and in relation to which [a th] every thing can alone be determined in distinction from others. If I represent to myself the understanding that thinks reality as light, and, insofar as it cancels it, as darkness, then one can think thoroughgoing determination either as a carrying of light here and there into the darkness, or the darkness as mere limitation of the universal light; and so things differ only through their shadows: reality lies at the ground, and indeed only a single universal one. In the opposite case all things differ only through their light, as if they had originally been raised out of darkness. But I can well think a negation if I have reality, but not if no reality is given. Thus reality is logically the first, and from this it is inferred that it is also metaphysically and objectively the first. M 42': Because objects of the senses are not given through the understanding (g and not a priori at all), here negation is the first, and the darkness out of which the light of experience works out shapes. Thus, in accordance with appearances, [is] are manifolds original, and unity arises when one abstracts from the manifoldness.",
    "reflm:5271": "In und The principle of thoroughgoing determination (principium omnimodae determinationis) is: every thing, with regard to its entire possibility, stands under the concept of the most real being (entis realissimi).",
    "reflm:5274": "1588. The principle of determination (principium determinationis) says that every thing is at all times determined with regard to one of contradictorily opposed predicates (praedicatis contradictorie oppositis). The principle of thoroughgoing determination (principium omnimodae determinationis) says that it is determined with regard to all of them together. The principle is. All negations in things are limitations. Consequently the being thoroughly determined a priori (ens a priori omnimode determinatum) is the most real being (ens realissimum), and lies at the ground of our concepts of the real possibility of things just as the principle of contradiction (princip contradictionis) lies at the ground of the logical. The former is the material principle, and all negations are only the formal element of things considered transcendentally.",
    "reflm:5554": "S. I: The concepts of reflection can be taken logically, hence merely [intellectually] analytically, or transcendentally, hence synthetically. Identity and difference, taken intellectually, is analytic, but determined sensibly, synthetic; and difference of place, regardless of the identity of the things in themselves, makes numerical difference. Agreement and conflict, logically, rest on the principle of contradiction (Satz des Widerspruchs); in empirical use: two realities that together yield a reality or a negation. The inner and outer, logically, is what is a predicate of the thing itself or of another; empirically: what is in another place in space. The determinable and the determined: the substantial and the substance. Of this the most famous proposition of subreption is: the principle of indiscernibles (principium indiscernibilium). Our three higher faculties aim at unity, truth and perfection, through understanding, the power of judgment and reason. The last, in the transcendental sense, produces all sorts of confusion. One can regard the whole monadology as the system ... of Leibniz from the concepts of reflection. The inner and outer taken intellectually yields the monad, because a composite (compositum) is a whole of relations (totum relationum), and representative (repraesentativa), because representations (repraesentationes) are inner, but place and extension outer. Space is nothing but the phenomenon of the outer. But what then do the monads represent, if they merely represent one another, each therefore the representations of the others? (Nor can one, with Maupertuis, derive the outer element of motion from the inner.) Leibniz. Preestablished harmony (harmonia praestabilita). Because the monads do not stand in community (in commercio) by virtue of coexistence in space. S. II: Noumenon means (g properly) everywhere the same thing, namely the transcendental object of sensible intuition (g This, however, is not a real object or given thing, but a concept in relation to which appearances have unity.), for to this [intuition] something must after all correspond, even though we know nothing other than its appearance. But we cannot say that the (g pure) categories have objects; rather they merely determine the transcendental object in relation to our sensibility through the synthesis of the manifold of intuition. Thus [is] no noumenon corresponds to them. The transcendental object that corresponds to appearances (g or indeed every object) can be called noumenon only insofar as it can be represented through the concept of the understanding. Now this is impossible through the category, since the conditions of intuition are lacking; thus we have no concepts for noumena. We can use these concepts by an analogy with the sensible [think]; but because they have objective validity only in relation to the synthetic unity of apprehension in time, in themselves they refer to no object at all, and under sensible determination only to phenomena.",
    "reflm:5556": "Analytic possibility rests on the principle of contradiction (Satze des Wiederspruchs) and is the possibility of the concept; synthetic possibility, which must be added, i.e. that an object can correspond to the concept, rests on this: that something accords with the conditions of an object of experience (g in general). (g Only in experience can an object be given.) The actual is opposed to the impossible and at the same time to the merely possible.",
    "reflm:5574": "Über und Whether something can be determined in another way or not, we see either from the principle of contradiction (principio contradictionis); and then, if it [cannot in anoth] can be determined, i.e. does not contradict itself, real contingency does not follow; or from alterations: but there not either.",
    "reflm:5686": "The principle of contradiction (Principium Contradictionis) is the principle of all logical possibility, i.e. of concepts, insofar as such can be cognized a priori, but not of things.",
    "reflm:5699": "The principle of reason (principium rationis) holds only of experience; for we can have no concept of a real ground except through experience, namely where, when something is posited, something else follows according to a rule (hence is determined). Thus from mere concepts we also can say nothing about the relation of things to real grounds.",
    "reflm:5722": "We can assume no object as possible except that which we can exhibit in intuition, consequently whose actuality we can display; otherwise, if the representation does not contradict itself, the thought is indeed possible, but not the thing. Logical (g inner) possibility (g according to the principle of contradiction (Satze des Wiederspruchs)) without real possibility (g to which no intuition corresponds): an empty concept. Impossibility we can cognize only according to the principle of contradiction when the talk is of objects to which no intuition can be given.",
    "reflm:5726": "17. E II 639. 597. Space and time. From the beginning, according to the distinction of analytic and synthetic cognition and the principle of contradiction (Satz des Wiederspruchs). Every object of thinking is either something or nothing. Either insofar as no thought corresponds to the object, i.e. the thinking itself is nothing, i.e. contradicts itself, or no object ((g none) in intuition) corresponds to the thought (which does not contradict itself). Thinking, considered subjectively as representation, before it is analysed, always has an object; but if the thinking contradicts itself, then [it] the thought and therefore the object is nothing, and both are struck out. Where the thought remains, there the object is, according to the analysis, problematic. Now something can be either [posited] merely posited or posited repeatedly (iterative) in order to bring about the representation of the object; in the latter case it is multiplicity, in the [second] first [unity] one. All multiplicity is therefore homogeneous, and repeated positing is addition. [This] The object whose representation arises through the multiplicity of given units is a quantum; and the representation of it as an object that contains many in itself is that of magnitude. In all magnitude there is composition. That [which] out of which something is composed is called unity and is comparatively (i.e. out of such unity) not composite, hence simple. Thus unities are relatively simple, but in themselves they can again be composite, i.e. magnitudes. An object of a certain kind is uniform in that same kind, e.g. space. But as multiplicity the unity can nevertheless be heterogeneous with the quantum, and the unity can be a quantum that is in turn composed of unities of an entirely different kind. Accordingly not every multiplicity is a magnitude, M 17: but only that which is homogeneous with the object in which the magnitude is considered. What a multitude is, what counting, what number, what an infinite magnitude is: given (is contradictory) or only infinite progression, by which the magnitude is determined only negatively. Space and time are quanta a priori. Continuous and discrete quantum (Qvantum continuum et discretum). Measure. The measure of magnitude in itself is allness; of the comparative, a given unity (in intuition). We know only comparative magnitudes. Bounds: space and time alone have them; magnitudes of pure thinking: limits. [That which] in thinking there are limits (Schranken), in intuition bounds (Grenzen). When the following category is at an end, it must be considered in connection with the preceding one. Immediately after the categories, the concepts of comparison. Identity and diversity; similarity, equality, congruence. Opposite and contrary (oppositum et contrarium). And after that the categories themselves compared. Of division in general.",
    "reflm:5811": "1319. In themselves things can determine themselves to the opposite (ad oppositum) neither by themselves (g alone) nor in community; for no predicate opposed to a subject belongs to it (nulli subiecto competit praedicatum ipsi oppositum), and ratio[cont] &lt;8A is also the ground (est etiam ratio) >8 of not-A (Non A). Thus time, in which alone alteration can be thought, is no [representation of the] determination of things in themselves; hence all alterations are merely determinations of appearances, and time itself, in which existence is determined as alterable, is not something that attaches to the existence of things in themselves, but merely to our kind of sensible representation (not of the understanding). Unalterable, therefore, is not merely the (g in itself) necessary being, but also everything intellectual. [In] In the objects of the senses, however, every [alteration necessary] determination that constitutes the alteration is necessary, but always only hypothetically, to infinity. The principle of contradiction (Satz des Wiederspruchs), insofar as it is restricted to what is in time, does not concern concepts of things, but [concerns] of mere appearances; and it is not contradictory that both opposed predicates (Pradicate opposita) (s only not at the same time) belong to the former as consequences in appearance, and neither of them to them as things in themselves. I, as object of sensible intuition, am alterable, and cannot bring the concept of myself under transcendental predicates of things in themselves, e.g. the simple or the composite etc., but can speak only of my intuition. But I, as subject of thinking and merely as object of reason, can in turn cognize no predicates through which such an object would be given to me in concreto: no extension, persistence in time, but also not their opposite. Thus all psychology as doctrine concerns only the human being as a thinking being, not the mere I in general; but psychology as critique prevents what holds of me, insofar as I am a human being, from being taken for the only thing that holds for me as a thinking being in general.",
    "reflm:5812": "Neben und The concept of the most real being (entis realissimi) is the ontological place for all possible things insofar as they differ from one another as things in general. Their difference then consists merely in the limitation of the concept of the totality of reality (omnitudo realitatis). Instead of thinking every thing as determinable, according to the principle of excluded middle (principio exclusi medii), with regard to the real of all predicates, or rather for the sake of this determinability, in order to represent it analytically, one takes something thoroughly determined as the ground of all.",
    "reflm:6206": "Th II: Human reason has the peculiar property, [not only the po] besides what belongs to making for itself a concept of a thing for a certain purpose, not only of completing this concept itself by means of everything that constitutes it, but also of completing the object of the concept within the kind of things to which it belongs. We are not content with what would suffice for the common use of words, to know distinctly the concept of a body, of a human being, of a plant; we seek [it] to become conscious of it in all its marks, and from this, when the law of parsimony is added, arises the definition. But we seek, moreover, once we have counted the object among a certain kind of things, to think it completely with regard to this kind. Body belongs to matter, and what in its extension is not matter is empty space; hence we make for ourselves the concept of a perfectly dense body. It has cohesion; we think to ourselves a perfectly hard one, without troubling ourselves whether such a thing is actual or even (g only) possible. Thus the completeness of a thing of a certain kind serves us only as the standard of measure for all the other concepts [things] that we could make of it, insofar as they differ from one another merely in magnitude. These magnitudes are variable; one must compare them with one that is invariable, i.e. with that of a thing which contains everything that can be contained in its concept in relation to its kind. Some of these concepts of completion we can determine, in that we have [all] a determinate concept, and in experience [a concept to be specified at least negatively], of what belongs to its completion (e.g. among all chords in the circle, the diameter); others are so constituted that we only think the completion, but cannot ourselves complete the concept. The concept of well-being is empirical, but much can still [be missing] be missing from contentment with one's condition. Here, now, a concept is needed in whose content nothing is lacking, i.e. the greatest and [best] everlasting well-being, i.e. happiness (g Th I: which we can nevertheless never think determinately). If this also does not depend on contingent external causes but springs from ourselves: blessedness. Now we can extend this concept of the satisfaction of a rational being still further, by [doing] adding to what it enjoys also what it does, i.e. contentment with its person and the moral good. The complete moral good is the highest virtue. If this is itself free of all inclination to evil: holiness. Holiness of the will, then, and blessedness of the condition together make up the idea of heaven. On the other side, because that [which not] whose concept consists in the relation of cause and effect has a twofold opposite: a negative =0 and a privative = –, one can think a condition that leaves no contentment at all: misfortune, and insofar as the being contains the cause in itself: misery; likewise also a will that acts intentionally against all moral laws, and thus arises the idea of hell. Hence heavenly joys and hellish torments, heavenly virtue and hellish vices } ideas. Th III: These ideas are entirely necessary, even if [the] no object corresponded to them. Nor does it matter whether such a thing exists, but only how we can better compare our concepts of what is with one another, by holding them up against a third, even though it is merely an idea, as a common measure. (The Julian period runs 532 years before the beginning of the world.) If we now think to ourselves an individual according to these ideas, it is the good or evil original being; for both are considered not according to what they contain in themselves but according to what they are the cause of. (Whether in the world there is as much evil on the one side as there is good on the other, and whether, just as motion cannot be imparted in one direction without producing or taking away just as much on the opposite side, law of isonomy (lex isonomiae), the principle would indeed be good, but its work would necessarily contain just as much evil as good.) Both would contain everything that is required for activity, but the one always the ground of reaction against the [activ] effect of the other. If the evil being is derived as to its existence, but is nevertheless, though a limited one, the cause of all ill: then it is the Evil One or the devil, an imprecise idea, of which one does not know how far its influence and effect extend. If we think a completed being not according to determinate concepts of good and evil but as a thing in general, then this is the transcendental idea of a highest being, which is also necessary in order for us to think the differences of all possible things in thoroughgoing determination (durchgängigen Bestimmung) with regard to something and nothing in general. First everything is thought in one, in order afterwards to think more in one thing, less in another, through mere limitation of the archetype. I always keep the same subject: a thing in general, and through various limitation of the being that contains everything positive, I obtain all things insofar as they are something, and the differences rest, as it were, on the combination of something with nothing in infinite space. The evil original being is also thought as infinite reality, but in its effect as the counterpart of all causality of the former, whereby the world then contains no contradictions, to be sure, but nothing but conflict of purposes. Besides the necessity of the idea of a most perfect being for the concepts of all others, reason [counts] judges it [also] to be necessary for the existence of things. These, considered merely as things, can differ only through [reali] negations, hence only through limits of a highest reality. Now the existence of limited things seems to be just as derivative as their concept, and on the other hand the existence of a being that has all reality alone seems able to be original. All shadows are only remnants of the infinite nothing, namely of the night which, without the all-illuminating sun, would fill space. It therefore seems natural that it is not light mixed with shadow that is there first, but that it arises only through greater or lesser limitation of sunlight, which must precede. The highest reality can limit its own effect, whereby it reveals its existence outwardly; but limited reality has in itself no sufficient principle of its possibility; consequently its actuality too is to be regarded as dependent. Further, from the principle of thoroughgoing determination there arises a thoroughgoing community of descent, hence affinity of everything possible, because it is possible only in one concept, since all negations are possible only through the delimitation of the highest reality.",
    "reflm:6207": "The principle of excluded middle (principium [Der du] exclusi medii), i.e. of determination, contains this: that if a concept is compared with one of two opposites (oppositorum), one of the two must belong to it. The principle of thoroughgoing determination (princip der durchgängigen Bestimmung) says that the concept of a thing in general, in order to make up the representation of an individual, must be compared with all possible opposite predicates (praedicatis oppositis), so that, once it has been determined with regard to one, it must in this determination be compared with other opposite predicates, and thus it can be thought as determined, as a thing in general, solely through its relation to the most real being (ente realissimo). Through this it comes about that a universal concept thoroughly determines itself and becomes the concept of an individual being.",
    "reflm:6208": "The principle of thoroughgoing determination (princip der durchgängigen Bestimmung) is a synthetic proposition; for it requires that the concept of every thing stand in community with the concepts of everything possible, and thus that to each determination there belong still others which do not belong analytically in the concept of the thing; consequently, that the universal concept be regarded only as a part of the concept which contains the predicates for everything possible.",
    "reflm:6209": "The principle of excluded middle (principium exclusi medii) says only that of two opposites (oppositis) not both can belong to a thing or be removed from it, but not that it must necessarily be compared with all possible predicates [oder auch], i.e. that it exists only in relation to them and to determination according to the principle of excluded middle. This is the principle of thoroughgoing determination (princip der durchgangigen Bestimmung). The first is logical and signifies only the nature of disjunctive judgments, which contain the opposition of contradiction (oppositionem contradictionis); the second is metaphysical and denotes the derivation of the concept of every thing from the sum total of all possibility, namely of the highest reality. The human understanding requires, for a determinate concept of a thing (namely not of that which is common to many things, but of an individual one), that it can thereby be distinguished from everything possible, because it judges only through universal concepts. It must therefore compare it with all possible predicates in the idea, and thinks the thoroughgoing determination, which yet no understanding is able to carry out. It therefore presupposes: in order to cognize a thing completely, one must know not only what it contains but, beyond that, everything that it lacks, so that one also cognizes it in relation. The principle of determination is that of excluded middle (exclusi medii). The principle of thoroughgoing determination says: every individual thing (i.e. insofar as it is distinguished from all others; for [das] a thing considered universally, as genus and species, is distinguished only from those which do not stand under this concept) is to be distinguished with regard to everything possible through its relation to the sum total of all possible predicates.",
    "reflm:6290": "30. ZuTh § 21: Th 30': That something is [possible] actual because it is possible according to a universal concept does not follow. But that something is actual because through its concept it is thoroughly determined among everything possible and is distinguished from everything possible as one, means as much as: it is not [mehr] merely a universal concept, but [der Begrif] the representation of an individual thing, thoroughly determined through concepts in relation to everything possible. This relation to everything possible according to the principle of thoroughgoing determination (princip der durchgangigen Bestimmung) is, according to concepts of reason, exactly the same as the somewhere or somewhen is according to the conditions of sensible intuition.* For space and time determine not merely the intuition of a thing but at the same time its individuality, through the relation of place and of point in time, because (g in) space and time possibility cannot be distinguished from actuality, for the reason that both together contain within themselves all possibility in appearance [mithin all] as substrates which must be given beforehand. From this it follows only that the most real being (ens realissimum) must be given in advance for the real concept of all possibility, and hence that, just as space cannot be thought beforehand as possible but only as given — yet not as [etwas] an object actual in itself, but as a mere sensible form in which alone objects can be intuited — so too the ens realissimum is to be thought not as an object, but as the mere form of reason for thinking, in its thoroughgoing determination, the difference of everything possible; consequently as an idea which is actual (subjectively) before anything is yet thought as possible; from which, however, it does not at all follow that the object of this idea is actual in itself. Nevertheless one sees that, in relation to the nature of the human understanding (g and its concepts), a highest being is just as necessary as space and time are in relation to the nature of our sensibility and its intuition. Th 30: *(g Something whose relation (g to everything possible) is determined in absolute space and time is actual. Likewise that whose relation to everything possible is determined in the absolute representation of a thing in general is actual. Both belong to the thoroughgoing external determination with regard to possibility in general and [also] thereby also make up the thoroughgoing internal determination of an individual (individui).)",
    "reflm:6291": "Formal ground of possibility — the principle of contradiction (principium contradictionis). Material ground of possibility — the principle of thoroughgoing determination (principium omnimodae determinationis). As sum total. The first concept of the absolute necessity of a being is (g logical, i.e.) this: that [seyn] its non-being contradicts itself. The 2nd concept of the absolute necessity of a being is metaphysical: that its non-being would cancel the possibility of all things. Proof of a common ground even of the possibilities of things from essential purposiveness.",
    "reflm:6298": "ZuTh § 23: We can ask: why anything at all is actual; for in doing so we presuppose possibility as independent of existence. But is the concept of possibility, then, also independent of all actuality and experience? The logical aspect of possibility is the principle of contradiction (Satz des Wiederspruchs), namely the possibility of the concept. But the possibility of things themselves requires more, namely synthesis. This is grounded in the actual nature of our sensibility and our understanding, and extends no further than to objects of possible experience. But even there the possibility, e.g. of a building where there is none, is nothing but the determination of a concept through all sorts of predicates, which, however, has not yet been extended to thoroughgoing determination, and hence not with regard to relations either. Complete possibility we can never have insight into, [aber] because we cannot determine a concept thoroughly and exhaustively in concreto. For if we could do this, there would belong to it predicates which cannot be taken from anywhere but experience, and whose meaning, too, consists only in the relation of the cognitive faculties to possible experience.",
    "reflm:6299": "ZuTh § 23: The cosmological proof: If something exists, then something or other exists absolutely necessarily, i.e. it must be impossible that it should not exist. If this means: it must contradict itself, then no being is absolutely necessary. If it means: I must necessarily presuppose its existence according to laws of reason (not as necessary), then it is necessity of hypothesis. Synthetic propositions a priori which do not refer to any object [de] of possible experience can never have objective necessity. But subjective necessity, that of reason's presupposition of a most real being (realissimo) as the substrate of all possibility [bey] in the principle of thoroughgoing determination (Grundsatz der durchgangigen Bestimung), they may well have, and that is sufficient for us. Reason always links one existence to another and can posit nothing for itself; it is therefore only the faculty of connections a priori, not of cognizing absolute necessity in existence. Nevertheless absolute necessity is an unavoidable presupposition of reason for its totality. It is therefore subjectively, not dogmatically, necessary.",
    "reflm:6328": "6. S. I: That a thing can be another than a certain given thing, although it is internally determined in exactly the same way — is contrary to the principle of the identity of indiscernibles (principium identitatis indiscernibilium). But that a thing can be the very same, although it is internally determined in entirely opposite ways, is contrary to the principle of the diversity of discernibles (principium diuersitatis discernibilium). — According to the latter, the proposition: Caius, who is (was) unlearned, is (now) learned, is a proposition which expresses the identity of the subject with (g mutually) opposed predicates of it in time, hence a proposition of the possibility of alteration, where, then, the predicate (learned) holds neither of the thing nor of its concept, but according to the state in which I posit (\"Sätze\") it, which (g in itself) is no concept of the thing.",
    "reflm:6377": "According to the principle of reason (principio rationis), the same state is always accompanied by the same consequence; because we can determine [durch] the state only through the consequence, the state determines the consequence. We cannot have insight into how something must follow upon something else; but that the same antecedent (antecedens) must have the same consequent (conseqvens) flows from this: that the state itself is determined through the consequence, and otherwise there would be no continuum of appearances (phaenomenorum) without connection of the members, and it is through this alone that they are also represented in a series.",
    "reflm:6381": "14. E II 1662. Existence contains within itself at the same time the concept of a thoroughgoing determination (determination); but through it (g alone) the concept of the thing is not determined at all. Now we have a concept which at the same time contains the thoroughgoing determination of a thing: the concept of the most real being (realissimi); and these determinations (so it seems to us) we can also [gege] specify in concreto, e.g. wisdom, power, etc. Only the existence of the thing (because this is not a determination) is not thereby given as following necessarily from this concept. But even if the necessary existence of some thing or other is assumed, I cannot say that the thoroughgoing determination with regard to its quality is given through the concept of necessity; rather it is given only through that of existence, where the predicates remain undetermined. There could be several necessary beings distinct from one another; but then, to be sure, none of them [Existenz] would be determinable from the concept of necessity (as to how it is constituted). One could only say: M 14: we have no concept for its thoroughgoing determination other than that of the most real being (realissimi), but not: this concept is contained in that of its necessity. It is only a subjective [Nothwend] logical necessity of the condition for forming for ourselves a concept of its determinations; but that if from the concept of it (of a non-most-real being, non realissimi) this idea is determinable in a twofold way (duplici modo), this means only: we can think many kinds of beings as necessary, not: the same being could also be determined otherwise, for here we have only concepts.",
    "reflm:6383": "If I posit something only relative to my concept, it is in many ways undetermined; but if it is posited absolutely, it is thoroughly determined according to the principle of excluded middle (principio exclusi medii); but I cannot infer conversely.",
    "reflm:6393": "On, alongside, and Absolutely Necessary Being (Absolute Necessarium Ens). That something is possible we cannot cognize through the mere non-contradiction of the thought, but we can indeed cognize from contradiction that something is impossible. That a thing is necessary, however, we can have insight into only through the contradiction of its opposite (either where the concept contradicts itself, or where its existence contradicts other given laws); where, therefore, nothing of the kind can be demanded, e.g. that the non-being of a thing contradict itself, there we cannot have insight into the impossibility of its non-being, and hence not into its necessity. If we cognized the possibility of all things intuitively, we would cognize the impossibility of something even without contradiction, by not encountering the thing at all in the field of possibility. Absolute necessity would be nothing but the intuition of a thing a priori. We are compelled to assume all things as necessary (as absolutely or hypothetically). The ground is [st], because we must represent everything true at the same time as logically necessary (principle of excluded middle, principium exclusi medii), since we determine things only through judgments, and judgments a posteriori, without being confirmed by such a priori, are not objective.",
    "reflm:6403": "In every alteration the substance persists, because alteration is the succession of the determinations of one and the same thing. This is a merely logical proposition according to the rule of identity (Regel der identitaet). But it does not say that substance in general does not arise or pass away, but only that it remains during the alteration. That proposition, (g however,) also holds only of (g corporeal) substances in space. For there, arising or passing away is always an alteration of what is not substantial, namely of space, which remains but is not an object of experience at all; consequently that alteration would also be no object of experience, hence no alteration. The persistence of matter through all change of relations [ist] is proved by this: that matter itself is a mere sum total of relations, which cannot fall away without all other relations falling away too, hence all possible outer experience.",
    "refl:1634": "The first principal division of philosophy is 1. that in which grounds are cognized through a consideration abstracted from the senses and from the distinct images of the imagination, thus through the pure intellect (intellectum purum); 2. that which takes grounds immediately by means of the comparison of sensible representations. The first is philosophy in the proper sense, the second mathematics. These explanations determine the specific mark of both sciences. We can, however, compare sensible representations with one another for various purposes. But with respect to grounds we can institute no other comparison that would lead [darauf führen solte] immediately, through the senses, to the cognition of the ground than insofar as it concerns magnitude. Consequently mathematics is the only science in which a distinct insight into grounds depends immediately on the senses or on their deputy, the imagination. [e.g. When I feel that the fire burns.] For the cognition of magnitude is nothing other than bricht ab. (g Mathematics is therefore not treated as a particular science because it is too large.) One might perhaps say: philosophy is not sufficiently distinguished from mathematics by its definition, because the latter also considers the grounds of [Gründe] things, namely insofar as one considers their magnitude. Should one not then say: the mathematician has a philosophical cognition of the relations of magnitudes? He does prove, after all? Here it is to be considered that in mathematics the ground and [deßen] the dependence of the relations on this ground are derived and cognized through a series of sensible comparisons, or of such representations as can all be made sensible. E.g. what relation all three angles in a triangle have to [denen Winkeln] two right angles, which can be comprehended in a semicircle. This, together with the ground, one can have insight into by drawing on the board all the concepts leading to it and making them sensible. But philosophy requires such an insight into grounds that their connection with what is derived from them is not grasped through an immediate sensible series of representations, and consequently is grasped through the pure intellect (per intellectum purum). If, e.g., I ask: why [nach dem] the moon is eclipsed at certain times, I cannot have insight into this otherwise than by having presupposed: a body which does not have its own light, but is exposed to another light, cannot be darkened otherwise than if something either covers it or something else prevents the light from falling on it, etc. These ideas cannot be made wholly sensible. But if I ask about the magnitude: how long the eclipse lasts, I can represent everything on the board from the given relations of magnitude, which I express through numbers. Hence mathematical cognition is to be distinguished from philosophical. A philosophical cognition of magnitudes and their relations is quite different. [E.g. when I ask: why why a figure cannot have [mehr] (g fewer) than 3 sides,] I know, e.g., with certainty that the circle is a figure which with its circumference encloses the greatest space that it can enclose with this circumference. (g Geometry gives the proof.) But if I ask: why, then, must the figure which encloses the greatest space etc. be so constituted that it is similar to itself through and through? This would be a philosophical question; the very same with perpendicular lines. A philosophical cognition of geometrical and arithmetical problems would be excellent; it would pave the way to the art of invention. But it is very difficult. E.g. that, if from a point I draw another line onto a line such that it makes nothing but equal angles, this is the shortest of all possible ones, geometry proves; but why must one hit upon precisely this determination in order to draw the shortest? That only a sublime philosophy can show. From this one can also see why mathematical cognition is more certain than philosophical. Pure mathematics (mathesis pura) needs only the principle of contradiction (Satz des Wiederspruchs), applied mathematics (applicata) sometimes that of sufficient reason (vom Zureichenden Grunde). Division of philosophy into theoretical and practical (Divisio Philosophiae in Theoreticam et practicam).",
    "refl:2093": "The perverse application of the principle [des Wiederspruchs] of sufficient reason (Satz des zureichenden Grundes), of the best world, of the principle of indiscernibles (principii indiscernibilium). O ornament of the best world etc. Further L 21' from β1−κ3: E.g. mathematical method.",
    "refl:2142": "Error and truth are only in judgments (s and indeed when they are thought as propositions. The geometer says: Suppose there were in a ® more than one right angle (angulus rectus).) Judgments are actions of the understanding and of reason. Truth is agreement of the understanding and of reason. The understanding alone does not err* (s because it cannot conflict with its own laws). Nor do the senses (s because they do not judge at all). The grounds of error must lie in other powers [auch nicht]. Pure reason does not err. But the human ⟨understanding⟩ can have no cognition without intuition, which is sensible. In logic sensibility is merely the subjective element of cognition, insofar as it does not agree with the objective, as precipitancy, confusion, habit, miscalculation. Logic has to do only with the agreement of cognition with itself, both according to the principle of contradiction (principio contradictionis) and according to that of reason (rationis). *(g Nor does it judge alone, i.e. independently; for all the conditions which supply (subministriren) it with one cognition or another mix themselves into it. In the human being it is partly passive. Some things are supplied (suppeditirt) to it, others withheld. Subjective conditions. One must become conscious of what one thinks in the concepts; and this comes from the state of inner sense, whether its horizon is clear or clouded. Sensory stimulus. Thus sensibility is a power that is always required for judging; the understanding the other; both make the oblique motion along the diagonal, in which there is something true and also something false.) (g As in calculating: good rules, but slips in the execution.)",
    "refl:2167": "The principle of contradiction (principium contradictionis): possibility of the judgment. Of reason (rationis): truth; assertoric. Of disjunction (disjunctionis): necessity; apodictic, because the opposite is impossible.",
    "refl:2172": "The (g logical) criterion of truth in logic is, first, that of possibility according to the principle of contradiction (Satz des Wiederspruchs); secondly, that of logical actuality according to the principle of sufficient reason (Satz des Zureichenden Grundes). Possible and grounded. The historical criterion of truth bricht ab?",
    "refl:2178": "From the consequence one can indeed infer a ground, but without determining it; but only from the sum total of all consequences can one infer a determinate ground, namely that this is the true ground. This allness cannot be cognized apodictically.* Through the principle of contradiction (Satz des Wiederspruchs) I can infer positively the possibility of cognition (not of things), negatively the impossibility of cognition and of things, indirectly the necessity of cognition and of things. As a negative principle it is universal; as a positive one, whether direct or indirect, it is not sufficient as a criterion of truth. (g The third principle is that of excluded middle (exclusi medii): where truth is proved through the falsity of the opposite.) *(s The consequence can be a negative sufficient criterion; namely, if a false consequence flows from a cognition, then the cognition is false; but not: if no false consequence flows, it is true. Hypothesis.) *(s Thus there are three logical (g formal) ways of finding truth: 1) the principle of contradiction and of identity (principium contradictionis et identitatis); 2. the principle of reason (principium rationis); 3. the principle of excluded middle between 2 contradictories (principium exclusi medii inter 2 contradictoria).)",
    "refl:2183": "To the objective criteria belong the internal (principle of contradiction, Satz des Wiederspruchs) or the external. Grounds and consequences.",
    "refl:2184": "The logical criterion of possibility is the principle of contradiction (Satz des Wiederspruchs): that the thought is possible.",
    "refl:2894": "The sphere of notions [max] is infinite and vanishing. The higher concept is contained in the lower, and the latter under the higher; [ergo] hence the dictum de omni et nullo (the principle of all and none). The highest concept contains the fewest things in itself, the lowest the most, namely thoroughgoing determination (omnimodam determinationem). The higher the concept, the more things I have abstracted from; the lower, the more I have determined. Therefore the highest is the most abstract, the lowest or singular the most concrete. (g notio from νουσ: mind.)",
    "refl:3030": "All division is either according to [einem] the principle of analytic judgments or of synthetic ones. The former according to the principle of excluded middle (principio exclusi medii), the latter according to the principle of ground (Princip des Grundes) in the connection of the diverse. The first is dichotomy, the second polytomy. [Bey synthetischen Sätzen a priori] In the division of concepts [von einer] which contain or prescribe a synthesis a priori, either the synthesis is divided or a synthetic concept a priori is divided; both belong to transcendental division. The first is tetrachotomy, the second trichotomy. Logical division is partition (partitio), because all members of the division come in pairs. The second is logical decomposition (decompositio logica): where, first, something is posited; secondly, something else is posited in addition or cancelled; thirdly, the composition of both is represented. The synthetic unity of consciousness is the transcendental ground of the possibility of synthetic judgments a priori. I connect, namely, A with consciousness. Then B (either represented merely as non-A, or also as something that is added to it). Thirdly the [Verbindung be] unity of both kinds of distributive consciousness in a collective one, i.e. in the concept of a thing. Thus, first, the analytic unity of the consciousness of A and non-A (=B), and then the synthetic unity of both. But there is also this copula bricht ab.",
    "refl:3031": "Polytomy can[not] not be taught in logic; for this requires cognition (g of the object) of the understanding as to its content; dichotomy, however, needs only the principle of contradiction (Satz des Wiederspruchs), without knowing the concept one wants to divide as to its content. Polytomy requires intuition, either a priori, as in mathematics: conic sections, or empirical, as in the description of nature. Yet [eine synthetische] division [der] from the principle of synthesis a priori has a trichotomy: 1. the concept [als] of the condition, 2. of the conditioned, 3. of the derivation of the latter from the former.",
    "refl:3063": "(g Quality of judgments: affirmative or negative.) The relation of concepts. (exponent): The [praed] ground — subject to predicate of the consequence — whole — part } form of judgments. } categorical, hypothetical, disjunctive. [Das Moment] Quantity. Universal, particular, singular. The modality. Problematic, assertoric, apodictic. It is of great importance to know which propositions, expressed problematically, I can use sufficiently. E.g. there can be a future life. For problematic propositions are often indisputable, although assertoric ones are exposed to objections. Likewise assertoric ones, even though they are not apodictic. E.g. empirical propositions. Problematic propositions as grounds of other truths are called hypotheses. The proposition: some men are not learned (qvidam homines non sunt eruditi) can be expressed by fig. 1 (not all). The infinite proposition: some are non-learned (qvidam sunt non-eruditi) by fig. 2 (some not). The concept of the learned is restricted with regard to men, i.e. narrower than the[selbe] latter. That the learned and the non-learned [alle] together make up all men, and hence that men are thought through the [Nicht] learned, but with a restriction. The learned are either considered as if they all stand under the concept of men, but only as a part of its sphere (g the other part is not learned). [Der B] Or [die M] only a part of men is considered as if it [unter] made up one sphere with the concept of the learned. The latter restricts the concept of men. [ersteres] (g Through negative predicates I place my understanding outside a determinate sphere, in an infinite space.) The negative proposition indicates that something is not contained under the sphere of a given concept; the infinite one: that something is contained under the sphere which lies outside the given concept; consequently [daß etw] it presupposes that outside its sphere there is another in which it is contained, hence that it belongs to a sphere which restricts the former. Fig. 2. The first takes place according to the principle of excluded middle (principio exclusi medii) (g between a and non-a there is no third) etc. The second according to that of thoroughgoing determination (durchgangigen determination), which is infinite. The first is the principle of determination: of two opposed judgments one is true. It says only that the proposition \"anima non est mortalis\" (the soul is not mortal) is opposed to the proposition \"anima est mortalis\" (the soul is mortal). The second takes place according to the principle of thoroughgoing determination (princip der durchgängigen Bestimmung), which, while it is to take place with regard to a thing in general, determines only with regard to thinghood in general, i.e. reality, and adds, outside the sphere of a concept, an infinite sphere of the determination of all things, namely of thinghood, i.e. reality. Outside the sphere of a concept there is room for an infinity of spheres. The proposition \"anima [non] est non-mortalis\" (the soul is non-mortal) is a judgment of determination, which says that of two opposed predicates a and non-a the latter belongs to the soul. Judgments of determination are all infinite [und sind von], in order to determine a thing thoroughly, not merely to indicate the relation of connection or of conflict. Logic does not look to the content [dadurch], i.e. the determination of the concept, but only to the form of the relation: agreement or conflict.",
    "refl:3072": "According to the principle of excluded middle (principium exclusi medii), the sphere of a concept, relative to another, is either exclusive or inclusive.",
    "refl:3132": "(s Synthetic (g intuitive) judgments a priori.) Axioms are intuitive judgments a priori (iudicia intuitiva a priori). (g The principle of contradiction (Satz des Wiederspruchs) is a discursive judgment (iudicium discursivum).) Formal principles are axioms; material ones are elementary propositions. (s Analytic principles are not axioms, because they are discursive. Synthetic ones, too, are axioms only when they are intuitive. Hence philosophy has no axioms, but acroamatic principles.)",
    "refl:3163": "In logic, the principle of reason (principium rationis). Further L 94 from ψ−θ: Episodic part.",
    "refl:3172": "particular negative (particulariter negans). On »E«: universal negative (vniversaliter negans). On »I«: particular affirmative (particulariter affirmans). Beside L §. 343 sentence 2 from µ−φ: According to the principle of excluded middle (principio exclusi Medii).",
    "refl:3217": "A mark of a mark is a mark of the thing itself (Nota notae est nota rei ipsius) (g is the principle of categorical syllogisms): this is the dictum and at the same time the ground of the first figure (prima figura).",
    "refl:3218": "The principle of categorical syllogisms (Vernunftschlüsse) is the dictum de omni et nullo (what is said of all and of none): A mark of a mark is a mark of the thing itself (Nota notae est nota rei ipsius): what contradicts the mark (repugnans notae) etc. That of hypothetical ones is: from the ground to the consequence the inference is valid (a ratione ad rationatum valet conseqventia), and likewise from the negation of the consequence to the negation of the ground. That of disjunctive ones is: from the negation of one of contradictorily opposed members (g to the affirmation of the other) the inference is valid, and [viceversa] from the positing of one etc. (not as in hypothetical ones, where [only (g the) convers] only one mode of inference held in the conversion).",
    "refl:3219": "Three principles for the 3 kinds of inference: 1. Principle of contradiction (principium contradictionis). 2. of reason (rationis) 3. of excluded middle (exclusi medii) — —",
    "refl:3239": "The middle term (medius terminus) must take the place of an intermediate mark (nota intermedia), hence stand first in the place of the subject (loco subiecti), secondly in the place of the predicate (loco praedicati). Hence the 1st figure tacitly lies at the ground of all syllogisms. (g NB. A mark of a mark is a mark of the thing itself (Nota notae est nota rei ipsius): this is the principle of the first figure and yet at the same time of all syllogisms (Vernunftschlüsse).)",
    "refl:3313": "E.g. the principle of sufficient reason (Satz des Zureichenden Grundes). On L §. 410 »saltus illegitimus« (illegitimate leap): There is order in the world; therefore there must be an author located outside the world.",
    "refl:3463": "Let one assume it and show its absurd consequences. Opposite L §. 504 »wir — Feh« (84930-85016): Doubter. — — — He who denies the principle of sufficient reason (Satz des zureichenden Grundes).",
    "blomberg:blomberg-s06-078": "The principle of identity (Satz der Identitaet) is the formal mark of truth in affirmative judgments, and the principle of contradiction (Principium Contradictionis) in negative ones.",
    "blomberg:blomberg-s08-622": "All analytic propositions, where the predicate lies in the notion of the subject, fall under the law of identity. But synthetic propositions, whose predicate lies not in the notion of the subject but in a mark that is distinct from the subject, do not fall under the principle of identity (Principium Identitatis) and of contradiction (Contradictionis). This principle relates only to analytic propositions; in synthetic propositions a predicate is added to the notion of the subject which is not contained in it. And all propositions that have this character can only be cognized intuitively, not through reason. Through reason one can cognize no coordination at all, for reason only subordinates. All synthetic propositions are either immediate propositions of experience, or intuitive propositions which are produced by the understanding and do not lie in the notion of the subject. Analytic propositions, however, can be drawn out of the concept of the subject by analysis (per analysin). For they are contained in the notion of the subject, and subordinated to it. Here reason can busy itself, and dissect the subordinate concepts and lay them out apart from one another. E.g. gold does not rust. This is a synthetic proposition; it is evident merely from immediate experience, and cannot properly be derived from the notion of gold by analysis (per analysin) at all. If something is to be postulated, it must therefore be so constituted that it convinces absolutely everyone. In a proof, falsity as to the matter is only accidental, but as to the form essential. False matter can be corrected, whereas false form is wholly incorrigible. Further, falsity of form is also far more widespread and more general than falsity of matter. The proton pseudos (first falsehood), where someone infers according to absurdity, or also assumes false propositions, likewise lies in the form, for it is remarkable that the form is subject not only to many but even to incorrigible errors.",
    "blomberg:blomberg-s11-015": "All indemonstrable judgments, insofar as they are the ground of all judgments, are called principles (Grund Satze), and they are either theoretical or practical. A practically indemonstrable proposition is called a postulate (Postulatum). All these principles are either material, namely insofar as they have a middle term (medium terminum), or formal, which have no middle term. The latter have no determinate predicate at all, but bear merely on the relation of predicates to subjects in general. To these now belong the principle of contradiction (Principium contradictionis) and the principle of identity (Principium Identitatis). For in these there is no determinate subject and predicate, or no determinate case, but it can be applied to any subject and predicate. Now in our judgments we have a twofold form, namely the synthetic and the analytic form; the principle of contradiction is now only analytic.",
    "blomberg:blomberg-s12-003": "An adequate inference consists in a syllogism (Vernunft Schluß), whose rule is this: a mark of a mark of a thing is a mark of the thing itself (nota notae rei est nota rei ipsius), and this is also the fundamental rule of all syllogistic, and at the same time the whole difference between a judgment and a syllogism. For to a judgment (Judicio) there belongs an immediate clear mark, but to a ratiocination (Ratiocinio) in addition a mediate clear mark. Consequently ratiocination is the relation of the remote mark (nota remota), through a mediate or intermediate mark (nota mediata, vel intermedia), to a certain given concept of a thing. In a ratiocination, therefore, these 3 acts occur: 1st, the proximate or intermediate mark (Nota proxima oder intermedia) is compared with the remote mark; 2, the proximate mark is compared with the thing; and 3rd, the remote mark is compared with the thing itself. The intermediate mark is also called the middle term (medius Terminus). Now since in every judgment the predicate constitutes the major term (majorem Terminum), we can say: in every syllogism there are 3 terms. 1. the proposition which I am to cognize by means of the intermediate mark, 2: the remote mark, and 3rd the middle term, or the intermediate mark, which contains within itself the ground of the relation of the thing to the remote mark. And these make up the 3 terms, namely the major, the minor and the conclusion (Majorem, minorem et conclusionem). In every syllogism, 1. the major term is compared in the middle term, or the proximate mark with the remote mark, 2 the middle term with the minor, or the subject, and finally 3: the minor with the major, or the predicate.",
    "blomberg:blomberg-s12-006": "1: A mark of a mark of a thing is a mark of the thing itself (Nota Notae rei est nota rei ipsius)",
    "blomberg:blomberg-s12-009": "What contradicts a mark of a thing contradicts the thing itself (Repugnans Nota Notae rei, repugnat rei ipsi). This now is the fundamental rule of all syllogisms.",
    "blomberg:blomberg-s12-010": "The logicians have a certain dictum de omni et nullo (what is said of all and of none), which they take to be the very first ground of all ratiocinations (ratiociniorum). But these fundamental rules cited here themselves precede even this.",
    "blomberg:blomberg-s12-011": "The dictum de omni et nullo runs thus among the logicians: what belongs to a universal concept belongs also to all concepts that are contained under it.",
    "wiener:wiener-s04-073": "The agreement of cognition with itself, irrespective of the objects, is a formal criterion of truth. For this makes a cognition possible as cognition. Without it no cognition at all is possible. Hence it is always indispensable, although it is not sufficient for the material criterion. It is a conditio sine qua non (condition without which not), e.g. the principle of contradiction (Satz des Widerspruches) is a formal criterion of truth. But whoever presents a logic so finely that it agrees with itself, for him this criterion is not sufficient. The answer to the question: what is truth? would thus be the agreement of a cognition with itself, and this is one ground for holding it to be true. A universally sufficient criterion of truth cannot be demanded. – If I abstract from all the various objects, then I retain only a concept of the object. A universal criterion of truth deals only with the form of thinking, which is not the agreement of cognition with the object, but with itself.",
    "wiener:wiener-s04-076": "2.) affirmation (affirmatio), when cognitions hang together as grounds with their consequences. If, e.g., I cite as the ground of the wetness of the west winds that they come over the sea, then that is one ground of truth. It belongs to the internal criterion of truth that a cognition be grounded. A criterion which rests on the principle of sufficient reason (principio des zureichenden Grundes) must always infer from the ground to the consequence.",
    "wiener:wiener-s04-085": "Since we abstract from all relation to the object, and consider only the rule of the agreement of the understanding with itself, the criterion of truth in logic can only be the agreement of the laws of cognition with themselves. The rule of the agreement of cognition with itself is the principle of contradiction (Satz des Widerspruches) and the principle of sufficient reason (Satz des zureichenden Grundes), which are indeed not sufficient materially (materialiter), but are sufficient for judging formally (formaliter) of a thing whether it agrees with itself. The principle of contradiction is a negative proposition. A cognition is false if it contradicts itself. But a cognition that does not contradict itself is not on that account true either. The principle of contradiction is indeed such that nothing can be contrary to it, but as a positive principle for cognizing truth it is not sufficient. It is positive insofar as through it I can see the necessity of truth in a necessary cognition. – The principle of sufficient reason is when a cognition hangs together with a ground, or when nothing but true consequences follow from a cognition. Both principles thus press toward unity. – If a cognition does not contradict itself, but its opposite would contradict itself, then it is true. If a cognition does not contradict itself, it is possible, but from the principle of contradiction (principio contradictionis) not all the unity of a cognition can yet be inferred. E.g. that my thoughts penetrate into the souls of other men is possible, but not on that account true. If one cognition is the ground of another, then there is a connection of the two cognitions. If the ground is true, then the consequence is also true. If all consequences are true, the ground must also be true. The principle of contradiction is treated deliberately in metaphysics, and also that the criterion of truth is the principle of reason (principium rationis).",
    "wiener:wiener-s04-090": "2.) positive. In cognizing the truth of a proposition it will not do for me to infer from the truth of some consequences to the truth of a cognition. The cognition can have something true in it, and from that arises the truth of this single consequence. But the rest can be false. Hence I cannot infer the truth of the cognition from such a ground. If indeed all consequences are false, then there is nothing true and nothing sound in the cognition. The positive inference is thus the burden of proof (onus probandi), where I must show the truth of all consequences. With the negative one, however, I need only show the falsity of some consequences, and hence this is a sufficient criterion. Only it is difficult, indeed it cannot happen at all, that I could know all the consequences. For if I knew them, then my cognition would be certain. Yet we use it, and in doing so make this probable inference: if most consequences are true, then they are all true. For if a cognition does not contradict itself, then I seek its ground and examine its consequences. If many, but not all, consequences can be found out, then I accept it as an incomplete cognition, inferring from the truth of various consequences to the truth of the proposition. For since it is impossible to show the truth of all consequences, it follows that here too we have no strictly sufficient usability of the criterion, but can only use it as a ground of probability. The principle of sufficient reason (Satz des zureichenden Grundes) is treated here not as in metaphysics, where it holds of the object, but as a logical proposition. For since the principle of contradiction (Satz des Widerspruches) is not yet a sufficient criterion of truth, the connection of cognition must nonetheless be investigated through it. The principle of contradiction is the principle of what subsists together, and of agreement; the principle of sufficient reason, the principle of connection.",
    "wiener:wiener-s11-012": "The principle of all possible contrarily opposed predicates (praedicatis contrarie oppositis) must come from the thing. This is the principle of thoroughgoing determination (durchgängige Bestimmung). But this thoroughgoing determination of a thing is impossible, because an infinite cognition is required to seek out all the predicates that belong to a thing, and I can therefore go on to infinity and still not thoroughly determine the thing. E.g. the soul is corporeal, not corporeal. The soul is mortal, not mortal. In logical use they can here count as affirmative.",
    "wiener:wiener-s11-024": "In categorical propositions, however, there is no settled condition. They are therefore essentially distinct judgments. In all hypothetical judgments I have two modes, a modus ponens and a modus tollens (modum ponentem et tollentem). The modus ponens says: if the antecedent is true, then the consequent is also true. The modus tollens says: if the consequent is false, then the antecedent is also false. This comes up later in the syllogistic.",
    "wiener:wiener-s12-002": "Immediate judgments (Judicia immediata) are 1) by equipollent judgments (per judicia aequipollentia): some men are not7 ° virtuous, consequently not all men are virtuous; nothing is without a ground and everything has its ground are substitutions. 2) Subalternate (Subalternata), also differing only in form, e.g. all men are mortal, consequently some; differing in quantity. 3) by opposed judgments (per judicia opposita): some men are learned, some not, differing in quality; some men not mortal, all men mortal, quantity and quality are a) contradictory (contradictoria) when one affirms or denies universally and the other denies or affirms particularly, i.e. the proper opposition, where the affirmation of the one cancels the other, in affirming one it denies the other (affirmando unum negat alterum), and conversely. (Thus proofs apagogically, indirectly; only one should see whether they are really contradictorily opposed (contradictoria opposita); in metaphysics one always comes up against the opposite",
    "busolt:busolt-s27-001": "To the chief rule of syllogisms (Vernunftschlüße) others otherwise count the principle of contradiction (Saz des Wiederspruchs): No judgment is true where the predicate contradicts the subject. But this principle of contradiction (Principium der Contradiction) cannot be used as a syllogism: for it is only a principle of judgment (Principium iudicii).",
    "busolt:busolt-s27-003": "a.) A mark of a mark is a mark of the thing itself (Nota notae est Nota rei ipsius); this holds of affirmative judgments.",
    "busolt:busolt-s27-004": "b.) What contradicts the mark contradicts the thing itself (Repugnans notae repugnat rei ipsi); this holds of negative inferences, e.g. being changeable is a mark of everything divisible, being divisible is a mark of a body, hence by means of divisibility changeability is a mark of the body.",
    "busolt:busolt-s27-006": "What contradicts the mark contradicts the thing itself (Repugnans notae repugnat rei ipsi). E.g. nothing composite is simple, all matter is composite, hence no matter is simple, etc. Being simple holds of composites, and by virtue of the composite of bodies. The two rules cited are just what the dictum de omni et nullo (what is said of all and of none) was for the ancients.",
    "busolt:busolt-s27-007": "a.) Dictum de omni: whatever holds of the genus or species holds also of all individuals contained in the genus or species.",
    "busolt:busolt-s27-008": "b.) Dictum de nullo: whatever contradicts the genus or species contradicts also all individuals contained in the genus or species.",
    "busolt:busolt-s29-022": "b.) The modus tollens (Modum tollentem); here I infer from the removal of the consequent to the removal of the antecedent (a remotione consequentis ad remotionem antecedentis), e.g. if there is blind chance, then there is no order in the world; but the latter is false, therefore the former; that is, modus tollens.",
    "busolt:busolt-s29-023": "If bodies are divisible, then they are changeable; but the former is true, therefore the latter. The major of the proposition says nothing more than how one proposition follows from the other. If the consequence is incorrect, then one says the inference is from the stick to the corner (a baculo ad angulum). The modus tollens takes place only from the consequent to the antecedent, namely when the consequent is removed the antecedent is removed (sublato consequente tollitur antecedens), but from the positing of the consequent to the positing of the antecedent the modus ponens is not valid (a positione consequentis ad positionem antecedentis non valet modus ponens). Disjunctive syllogisms consist in this, that the major is divided into several parts which are opposed to one another.",
  },
};
